OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026", "title": "The higher-dimensional Erdős distinct-distances conjecture", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "166", "family_title": "The higher-dimensional Erdős distinct-distances conjecture", "abstract_html": "For every fixed integer <i>d</i> ≥ 3, we prove that every set of <i>n</i> ≥ 2 distinct points in ℝ<sup><i>d</i></sup> determines at least \\(c_d n^{2/d}\\) distinct distances, where \\(c_d\\gt 0\\) depends only on <i>d</i>. This resolves the higher-dimensional Erdős distinct-distances conjecture positively.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem \\(1.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For a finite set \\(P\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\), define \\( \\Delta(P)=\\{|p-q|:p,q\\in P,\\ p\\ne q\\}. \\) For every integer \\(d\\ge3\\), there is a constant \\(c_d>0\\) such that every set \\(P\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\) of \\(n\\ge2\\) distinct …", "statement": "<p>For a finite set \\(P\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\), define\n\\[\n\\Delta(P)=\\{|p-q|:p,q\\in P,\\ p\\ne q\\}.\n\\]\nFor every integer \\(d\\ge3\\), there is a constant \\(c_d&gt;0\\) such that every set \\(P\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\) of \\(n\\ge2\\) distinct points satisfies\n\\[\n|\\Delta(P)|\\ge c_dn^{2/d}.\n\\]</p><p>The intermediate results below use the following conventions. Varieties are reduced complex algebraic varieties unless otherwise specified. Their degrees are the degrees of their projective closures. The degree of a pure-dimensional family is the sum of the degrees of its distinct irreducible components. For an affine set \\(T\\subset\\mathbb C^m\\),\n\\[\nH_T(D)=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\bigl(\\mathbb C[x_1,\\ldots,x_m]_{\\le D}/I(T)_{\\le D}\\bigr),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Env}_D(T)=Z(I(T)_{\\le D}).\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nH_{\\operatorname{Env}_D(T)}(D)=H_T(D).\n\\]\nFor projective sets, restriction rank refers to homogeneous polynomials of exact degree \\(D\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose the theorem fails, and take the least failing dimension \\(d\\ge3\\). There is a sequence with\n\\[\nN=|P|\\to\\infty,\\qquad\nM=1+|\\Delta(P)|,\\qquad\nB=N^{1/d},\\qquad A=B^2,\\qquad M/A\\to0.\n\\]\nMinimality provides the constant-factor assertions in dimensions \\(3\\le u&lt;d\\), and Proposition \\(5\\) supplies the planar input. Thus Proposition \\(6\\) applies.</p><p>Propositions \\(7\\)–\\(15\\) prove the sparse-cones assertion in this sequence regime. Proposition \\(20\\) then provides a positive-density symmetric selection with independent direction evaluations. Propositions \\(16\\)–\\(18\\) establish uniform concentration of evaluation flats. Proposition \\(19\\) supplies the dimension-three rich-motion deletion, with both orientations. Proposition \\(21\\) assembles these inputs into a nonempty equal-distance graph with the stated minimum degree and global and local concentration. Proposition \\(22\\) contradicts its existence.</p><p>Therefore there is no least failing dimension. For each fixed \\(d\\), the ratio\n\\[\n|\\Delta(P)|/|P|^{2/d}\n\\]\nhas positive infimum over finite sets of at least two distinct points: otherwise a sequence tending to zero would give the contradiction regime above. This positive infimum is the required \\(c_d\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Corollary \\(3.7\\), together with the interpolation and component-rank results used to prove it", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(Z\\subset\\mathbb C^m\\) be a locus defined by equations of degree at most \\(L\\ge1\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(Z\\subset\\mathbb C^m\\) be a locus defined by equations of degree at most \\(L\\ge1\\). For each \\(k\\), choose a subfamily of its \\(k\\)-dimensional irreducible components, of total degree \\(a_k\\), and let \\(V\\) be their union. Then\n\\[\nH_V(L)\\ge c_m\\sum_{k=0}^m a_kL^k.\n\\]\nFor an arbitrary reduced union \\(V\\) with dimension-by-dimension degrees \\(a_k\\), without a hypothesis on its defining equations,\n\\[\nH_V(D)\\le\\sum_{k=0}^m a_k\\binom{D+k}{k}.\n\\]\nIn particular, these conclusions apply to the components of \\(\\operatorname{Env}_L(T)\\).</p><p>The same component-rank conclusions hold projectively. For a union of lists of components of systems with respective degree bounds \\(L_\\nu\\), the lower estimates below hold with effective bound\n\\[\nL_*=\\sum_\\nu L_\\nu.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Restriction rank on the whole union is at least that on each pure-dimensional subfamily. Lemma \\(1.5\\) gives\n\\[\nH_V(L)\\ge c_ma_kL^k\n\\]\nfor every \\(k\\); taking a maximum loses at most the fixed factor \\(m+1\\). For the upper bound, restriction to \\(V\\) injects into the direct sum of restrictions to its pure-dimensional subfamilies. Apply Lemma \\(1.5\\) to each.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.1\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(P\\subset\\mathbb R^m\\) be finite and put \\(M=1+|\\Delta(P)|\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(P\\subset\\mathbb R^m\\) be finite and put \\(M=1+|\\Delta(P)|\\). Every \\(p\\in P\\) has a polynomial \\(s_p\\) of degree at most \\(2(M-1)\\) satisfying\n\\[\ns_p(p)=1,\\qquad s_p(q)=0\\quad(q\\in P\\setminus\\{p\\}).\n\\]\nIf \\(V\\subset\\mathbb C^m\\) is pure \\(u\\)-dimensional of degree \\(a\\), then\n\\[\n|P\\cap V|\\le C_maM^u.\n\\]\nMoreover, \\(P\\) is precisely the common zero set of equations of degree at most \\(2M+1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(p\\in P\\), set\n\\[\ns_p(x)=\\prod_{\\rho\\in\\Delta(P)}\n\\frac{\\sum_{j=1}^m(x_j-p_j)^2-\\rho^2}{-\\rho^2}.\n\\]\nEvery denominator is nonzero, and the stated evaluations follow.</p><p>For the sample bound, induct on \\(u\\) and add over irreducible components. Dimension zero is immediate from degree. If an irreducible positive-dimensional \\(V\\) contains \\(p\\in P\\), then \\(s_p\\) is nonzero on \\(V\\), and its proper section contains every other sample point of \\(V\\). That section has degree at most \\(2Ma\\). Induction gives the claimed bound after increasing the dimension-dependent constant.</p><p>Finally use\n\\[\n1-\\sum_{p\\in P}s_p(x),\\qquad\n(x_j-p_j)s_p(x)\\quad(p\\in P,\\ 1\\le j\\le m).\n\\]\nThese equations vanish on \\(P\\). At a common zero, the first forces some \\(s_p\\ne0\\), and the corresponding coordinate equations force \\(x=p\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(F\\) be algebraically closed, \\( R=F[Z_1,\\ldots,Z_c]_{(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_c)}, \\) and suppose \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_c\\in R\\) generate an ideal of finite colength.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(F\\) be algebraically closed,\n\\[\nR=F[Z_1,\\ldots,Z_c]_{(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_c)},\n\\]\nand suppose \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_c\\in R\\) generate an ideal of finite colength. If\n\\[\n(f_1,\\ldots,f_c)^t=Q(Z)(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_c)^t,\n\\]\nthen the class of \\(\\det Q\\) in \\(R/(f_1,\\ldots,f_c)\\) is nonzero.</p>", "proof": "<p>The coordinate parameters form a regular sequence. Since \\(R\\) is regular and Cohen–Macaulay, the \\(f_i\\), being a system of parameters, also form a regular sequence. Their Koszul complexes resolve \\(R/(f)\\) and \\(F\\). The matrix \\(Q\\) gives a chain map lifting \\(R/(f)\\to F\\), whose top map is multiplication by \\(\\det Q\\).</p><p>The coordinate Koszul resolution gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^i_R(F,R)=0\\quad(i&lt;c),\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Ext}^c_R(F,R)=F.\n\\]\nA composition series implies the same lower-degree vanishing for every finite-length module. Applying this to the kernel of \\(R/(f)\\to F\\) gives an injection\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^c_R(F,R)\\hookrightarrow\n\\operatorname{Ext}^c_R(R/(f),R).\n\\]\nUnder the Koszul identifications, \\(1\\) maps to \\(\\det Q\\), up to sign. Its image is nonzero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(F\\) be algebraically closed.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(F\\) be algebraically closed. Let \\(S\\subset F^c\\) be any selection of distinct isolated points of the common zero set of equations of degree at most \\(L\\ge1\\).</p><p>Each point of \\(S\\) has a separator of degree at most \\(c(L-1)\\), vanishing at every other common zero of the original system, including its positive-dimensional components. Arbitrary values and first partial derivatives on \\(S\\) can be interpolated at degree at most \\(C_cL\\). The set \\(S\\) itself is the exact zero set of equations of degree at most \\(C_cL\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix an isolated zero \\(z\\). Choose \\(c\\) constant linear combinations \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_c\\) of the original equations whose ideal has finite colength locally at \\(z\\). Successively avoid the finitely many minimal primes of preceding proper intersections; the forbidden coefficient vectors form a finite union of proper linear subspaces. These choices can be imposed simultaneously at any finite list of isolated points.</p><p>Telescoping coordinatewise gives\n\\[\nf(Y)-f(Z)=Q(Y,Z)(Y-Z),\n\\]\nwhere every entry has \\(Y\\)-degree at most \\(L-1\\). Work with coefficients in\n\\[\nA_z=F[Z]_{(Z-z)}/(f_1(Z),\\ldots,f_c(Z)).\n\\]\nFor every other original common zero \\(y\\), some coordinate of \\(y-Z\\) is a unit in \\(A_z\\). The adjugate identity therefore gives\n\\[\n\\det Q(y,Z)=0\\quad\\text{in }A_z.\n\\]\nOn setting \\(Y=z\\), Lemma \\(1.2\\), after translation, gives\n\\[\n\\det Q(z,Z)\\ne0\\quad\\text{in }A_z.\n\\]\nApply an \\(F\\)-linear functional nonzero on this element to the coefficients of \\(\\det Q(Y,Z)\\), and normalize. This yields the separator \\(s_z\\).</p><p>The explicit first-order interpolants are\n\\[\nu_z=3s_z^2-2s_z^3,\\qquad\nv_{z,j}=(Y_j-z_j)s_z^2.\n\\]\nThe first has value \\(1\\) and zero first derivatives at \\(z\\), and vanishes to first order at the other selected points. The second prescribes the \\(j\\)-th derivative at \\(z\\), with all other prescribed data zero. Their degrees are at most\n\\[\n\\max\\{3c(L-1),\\,2c(L-1)+1\\}.\n\\]\nThe equations\n\\[\n1-\\sum_{z\\in S}s_z,\\qquad (Y_j-z_j)s_z\n\\]\nisolate exactly \\(S\\). The empty set is isolated by \\(1=0\\).</p><p>The local quotient need not be reduced: the determinant records a nonzero top class, and the linear functional extracts a separator for the underlying point.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.5\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Suppose \\(S=\\bigcup_\\nu S_\\nu\\subset F^c\\), where each \\(S_\\nu\\) is a selection of isolated points of a system of degree at most \\(L_\\nu\\ge1\\).", "statement": "<p>Suppose \\(S=\\bigcup_\\nu S_\\nu\\subset F^c\\), where each \\(S_\\nu\\) is a selection of isolated points of a system of degree at most \\(L_\\nu\\ge1\\). Values and first derivatives on the distinct points of \\(S\\) can be interpolated at degree at most\n\\[\nC_c\\sum_\\nu L_\\nu.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(z\\in S\\), treat each list separately. If \\(z\\in S_\\nu\\), use its normalized separator. Otherwise, exact isolation from Lemma \\(1.3\\) supplies an equation for \\(S_\\nu\\) nonzero at \\(z\\), normalized there to \\(1\\). Their product separates \\(z\\) from the rest of the union, at degree \\(O_c(\\sum_\\nu L_\\nu)\\). Apply the first-order construction of Lemma \\(1.3\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.6\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(V\\subset\\mathbb C^m\\) be a union of distinct irreducible varieties, pure of dimension \\(k\\), with total degree \\(a\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(V\\subset\\mathbb C^m\\) be a union of distinct irreducible varieties, pure of dimension \\(k\\), with total degree \\(a\\). Then\n\\[\nH_V(D)\\le a\\binom{D+k}{k}.\n\\]\nIf every member is an irreducible component of a common locus defined by equations of degree at most \\(L\\ge1\\), then for some integer \\(q\\le C_mL\\),\n\\[\nH_V(D)\\ge a\\binom{D-q+k}{k}\\qquad(D\\ge q).\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\nH_V(L)\\ge c_maL^k.\n\\]\nOther components of the defining locus may have any dimension. The projective and union-of-systems versions stated in Proposition \\(1\\) also hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the upper bound, pass to the projective closure. Polynomial restrictions inject into \\(H^0(V,\\mathcal O_V(D))\\). A general hyperplane avoids associated components and has reduced pure-dimensional intersection of degree \\(a\\). The restriction sequence gives\n\\[\nh^0(V,\\mathcal O_V(D))\n\\le h^0(V,\\mathcal O_V(D-1))\n+h^0(V\\cap H,\\mathcal O_{V\\cap H}(D)).\n\\]\nDimension zero contributes its degree, and \\(h^0(V,\\mathcal O_V)\\le a\\). Induction on dimension and summation give the upper bound.</p><p>For the lower bound, choose generic linear coordinates\n\\[\n(t_1,\\ldots,t_k,y_1,\\ldots,y_{m-k})\n\\]\nso that projection to \\(t\\) is finite of degree \\(\\deg V_i\\) on each component. Over an algebraic closure \\(F\\) of \\(\\mathbb C(t)\\), the selected generic fiber has \\(a\\) distinct points. They are isolated in the fiber of the entire defining system: the intersection of a selected component with any different component projects nondominantly to the base.</p><p>Lemma \\(1.3\\) makes evaluation surjective from \\(F[y]_{\\le q}\\), \\(q\\le C_mL\\). Choose \\(a\\) ordinary \\(y\\)-monomials \\(m_i\\) whose evaluation columns are independent over \\(F\\). The polynomials\n\\[\nt^\\alpha m_i,\\qquad |\\alpha|\\le D-q,\n\\]\nare independent on \\(V\\): a complex relation would give an \\(F\\)-linear relation among the \\(m_i\\), with polynomial coefficients in the algebraically independent \\(t_j\\). Counting proves the shifted lower bound.</p><p>To return to degree \\(L\\), use standard monomials in a total-degree-refining monomial order. They are closed under division. The map\n\\[\nx^\\alpha\\longmapsto x^{\\lfloor\\alpha/b\\rfloor}\n\\]\nhas at most \\(b^m\\) preimages and sends degree at most \\(bL\\) standard monomials to degree at most \\(L\\). Hence\n\\[\nH_V(bL)\\le b^mH_V(L).\n\\]\nChoose fixed \\(b\\) with \\(bL-q\\ge L\\).</p><p>For several systems, use Lemma \\(1.4\\) on the selected finite lists in the geometric generic fiber. Only ordinary transverse monomials are selected; no rational coefficient is cleared.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Lemmas \\(3.8\\) and \\(3.9\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(V=\\bigcup_iV_i\\subset\\mathbb C^m\\) be a pure \\(k\\)-dimensional family of total degree \\(a\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(V=\\bigcup_iV_i\\subset\\mathbb C^m\\) be a pure \\(k\\)-dimensional family of total degree \\(a\\). Assume its members are components of a system of degree at most \\(L\\), or of lists of systems with effective bound \\(L=\\sum_\\nu L_\\nu\\).</p><p>First, for \\(D\\ge C_mL\\), the equations of degree at most \\(D\\) vanishing on the whole family have generic Jacobian rank \\(m-k\\) except on components of total degree at most\n\\[\nC_maL/D.\n\\]\nA finite set of these equations and a polynomial \\(g\\) of degree at most \\(C_mD\\), formed as a linear combination of maximal Jacobian minors, can be chosen so that \\(g\\) is nonzero on every retained component. At points of \\(V\\) with \\(g\\ne0\\), their common zero set is locally a smooth \\(k\\)-fold equal locally to \\(V\\); distinct members cannot meet there.</p><p>Second, if \\(h_i\\) is a proper polynomial cut on \\(V_i\\), with \\(\\deg h_i\\le L'\\), then for\n\\[\nD\\ge C_m\\max\\{L,L'\\}\n\\]\nthere is a polynomial of degree at most \\(D\\) vanishing on every \\(V_i\\cap Z(h_i)\\), but identically zero on components of total degree at most\n\\[\nC_maL'/D.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The two assertions are Lemmas \\(2.1\\) and \\(2.2\\), with constants depending only on the fixed ambient dimension.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.8\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the assumptions of Proposition \\(2\\), the first, common-regularity conclusion holds.", "statement": "<p>Under the assumptions of Proposition \\(2\\), the first, common-regularity conclusion holds.</p>", "proof": "<p>The ambient case \\(k=m\\) is immediate. Otherwise use the generic coordinates and geometric generic fiber of Lemma \\(1.5\\). Let \\(b\\) be the total degree of the failing components, contributing \\(b\\) of the \\(a\\) sheets. Set\n\\[\nW_D=\\mathbb C[t,y]_{\\le D},\\qquad\nE_D(f)=(f(t,z_i))_{i=1}^a.\n\\]\nThese are a vector space and map over \\(\\mathbb C\\), with\n\\[\n\\ker_{\\mathbb C}E_D=I(V)_{\\le D},\\qquad\n\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb C}E_D=H_V(D).\n\\]</p><p>At each failing sheet choose a nonzero vertical vector \\(v_i\\) in the common kernel of the gradients. The component tangent projects isomorphically to the base, so \\(v_i\\) is transverse. Put\n\\[\nJ_i(f)=v_i\\cdot\\partial_yf(t,z_i).\n\\]\nThese functionals annihilate \\(\\ker_{\\mathbb C}E_D\\); their algebraic coefficients are not differentiated. Consequently\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb C}(E_D,J)=H_V(D).\n\\]\nFirst-order interpolation on the generic fiber supplies \\(a+b\\) ordinary \\(y\\)-monomials of degree at most \\(q\\le C_mL\\), with independent augmented columns. Multiplication by base monomials gives\n\\[\n(a+b)\\binom{D-q+k}{k}\n\\le H_V(D)\\le a\\binom{D+k}{k}.\n\\]\nFor \\(D\\ge2q\\), the binomial ratio is at most \\(1+C_mq/D\\), proving the exceptional-degree estimate. For \\(k=0\\), it gives \\(b=0\\).</p><p>Take a basis of \\(I(V)_{\\le D}\\). A generic constant linear combination of all maximal Jacobian minors is nonzero on each retained component. Where it is nonzero, some \\(m-k\\) equations have independent differentials. The implicit function theorem gives a smooth \\(k\\)-fold; the contained pure \\(k\\)-dimensional germ of \\(V\\) equals it. Two distinct components cannot share that open germ.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.9\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the assumptions of Proposition \\(2\\), the second, pooled-proper-cut conclusion holds.", "statement": "<p>Under the assumptions of Proposition \\(2\\), the second, pooled-proper-cut conclusion holds.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(k=0\\), every cut is empty; take \\(g=1\\). Otherwise let \\(C\\) be the union of the reduced cut supports. Bézout and Proposition \\(1\\) give\n\\[\n\\deg C\\le aL',\\qquad\nH_C(D)\\le aL'\\binom{D+k-1}{k-1}.\n\\]\nCall \\(V_i\\) forced if every member of \\(I(C)_{\\le D}\\) vanishes on it. If their union has degree \\(b\\), the kernel inclusion gives\n\\[\nH_{\\bigcup_{i\\text{ forced}}V_i}(D)\\le H_C(D).\n\\]\nThe lower bound in Lemma \\(1.5\\), for \\(D\\ge C_mL\\), gives \\(b\\le C_maL'/D\\). Choose \\(g\\in I(C)_{\\le D}\\) outside the finitely many proper linear subspaces corresponding to unforced components.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemmas \\(3.10\\)–\\(3.12\\), Corollaries \\(3.13\\)–\\(3.14\\), and Lemma \\(3.15\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The following multiplicity, matrix-rank, and projection estimates hold in fixed ambient dimension.", "statement": "<p>The following multiplicity, matrix-rank, and projection estimates hold in fixed ambient dimension. Here \\(\\operatorname{mult}_pX\\) denotes Hilbert–Samuel multiplicity, set equal to \\(0\\) when \\(p\\notin X\\); for a hypersurface it is the order of its defining equation.</p>", "proof": "<p>The asserted estimates are Lemmas \\(3.1\\)–\\(3.6\\). Their proofs distinguish ambient polynomial order, local quotient length, and Hilbert–Samuel multiplicity as in the paper.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.10\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(S\\subset\\mathbb C^m\\) be finite and \\(x_*\\in\\mathbb C^m\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(S\\subset\\mathbb C^m\\) be finite and \\(x_*\\in\\mathbb C^m\\). If a polynomial \\(f\\) of degree at most \\(E\\) has order at least \\(r\\ge1\\) at every point of \\(S\\), and \\(f(x_*)\\ne0\\), then there is a polynomial \\(g\\) with\n\\[\n\\deg g\\le(m+1)E/r,\\qquad g|_S=0,\\qquad g(x_*)\\ne0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The empty case is immediate. First work over an algebraically closed field of characteristic \\(p\\ge r\\), and put \\(a=\\lceil mp/r\\rceil\\). Grouping monomials modulo \\(p\\), write uniquely\n\\[\nf(X)^a=\\sum_{0\\le i_1,\\ldots,i_m&lt;p}X^ih_i(X)^p.\n\\]\nEvery nonzero \\(h_i\\) has degree at most\n\\[\naE/p\\le mE/r+E/p\\le(m+1)E/r.\n\\]\nAt \\(z\\in S\\), the order of \\(f^a\\) is at least \\(mp\\), so\n\\[\nf^a\\in J_z=((X_1-z_1)^p,\\ldots,(X_m-z_m)^p).\n\\]\nModulo \\(J_z\\), \\(h_i(X)^p=h_i(z)^p\\), and the monomials \\(X^i\\) with coordinate exponents below \\(p\\) form a basis. Thus all \\(h_i(z)=0\\). At least one \\(h_i(x_*)\\ne0\\).</p><p>For transfer to \\(\\mathbb C\\), let \\(q=\\lfloor(m+1)E/r\\rfloor\\). If no degree-\\(q\\) separator exists, evaluation at \\(x_*\\) lies in the span of evaluations at \\(S=\\{z_1,\\ldots,z_s\\}\\):\n\\[\nx_*^\\alpha=\\sum_{j=1}^s\\lambda_jz_j^\\alpha\\qquad(|\\alpha|\\le q).\n\\]\nLet \\(R\\subset\\mathbb C\\) be the finitely generated \\(\\mathbb Z\\)-algebra generated by the data, the \\(\\lambda_j\\), and \\(f(x_*)^{-1}\\). All hypotheses and these evaluation identities are polynomial identities over \\(R\\).</p><p>Noether normalization over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), followed by inversion of one integer, makes \\(R\\) finite integral over a polynomial ring over \\(\\mathbb Z[1/a]\\). Lying over supplies specializations to algebraically closed fields of arbitrarily large positive characteristic. A specialization with \\(p\\ge r\\) preserves \\(f(x_*)\\ne0\\), but the positive-characteristic construction contradicts the specialized evaluation identity. Repeated specialized points cause no problem.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.11\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(A(x)\\) be a polynomial matrix and \\(F(x)\\) a \\(q\\times q\\) minor.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(A(x)\\) be a polynomial matrix and \\(F(x)\\) a \\(q\\times q\\) minor. If\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}A(p)\\le q-h,\n\\]\nthen \\(\\operatorname{mult}_pF\\ge h\\). If \\(F\\) has degree at most \\(E\\), is nonzero at \\(x_*\\), and this rank loss occurs on a finite set \\(S\\), Lemma \\(3.1\\) supplies a separator of \\(S\\) from \\(x_*\\) of degree at most \\((m+1)E/h\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand the determinant using columns from \\(A(p)\\) and \\(A(x)-A(p)\\). A term using fewer than \\(h\\) latter columns has more than \\(q-h\\) constant columns and is zero. Every remaining term lies in the \\(h\\)-th power of the ambient maximal ideal at \\(p\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.12\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(X\\subset\\mathbb P^m_{\\mathbb C}\\) be irreducible of dimension \\(k\\ge1\\), degree \\(a\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(X\\subset\\mathbb P^m_{\\mathbb C}\\) be irreducible of dimension \\(k\\ge1\\), degree \\(a\\).</p><p>If projection from \\(p\\) has \\(k\\)-dimensional image \\(Y\\) and generic degree \\(t\\), then\n\\[\nt\\deg Y=a-\\operatorname{mult}_pX.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(k&lt;m\\), a generic linear projection to \\(\\mathbb P^{k+1}\\) is finite and birational on \\(X\\), with irreducible hypersurface image of degree \\(a\\). Given finitely many \\(p_i\\in X\\), it may simultaneously be chosen with singleton fibers there and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{mult}_{\\pi(p_i)}\\pi(X)\\ge\\operatorname{mult}_{p_i}X.\n\\]\nGiven also \\(x_*\\notin X\\), it may be required that \\(\\pi(x_*)\\notin\\pi(X)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(p\\notin X\\), intersect pullbacks of general target hyperplanes to obtain \\(t\\deg Y=a\\). If \\(p\\in X\\), blow up its ideal:\n\\[\nb:\\widetilde X\\to X.\n\\]\nLet \\(H\\) be the pulled-back hyperplane class and \\(E\\) the exceptional Cartier divisor. The resolved projection has hyperplane class \\(H-E\\). Since \\(H\\) restricts trivially to \\(E\\),\n\\[\nH^{k-j}E^j=0\\quad(1\\le j&lt;k).\n\\]\nThe exceptional scheme is\n\\[\nE=\\operatorname{Proj}\\bigoplus_{j\\ge0}\\mathfrak m_p^j/\\mathfrak m_p^{j+1},\n\\qquad \\mathcal O(E)|_E=\\mathcal O_E(-1),\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nE^k=(-1)^{k-1}\\operatorname{mult}_pX.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nt\\deg Y=(H-E)^k=a-\\operatorname{mult}_pX.\n\\]</p><p>For the second assertion, choose a general center\n\\(\\Lambda\\simeq\\mathbb P^{m-k-2}\\), avoiding \\(X\\), the joins \\(J(p_i,X)\\), the join from one chosen smooth point \\(q_0\\), its tangent space, and, if specified, \\(J(x_*,X)\\). Their dimensions make these nonempty open avoidance conditions. A positive-dimensional projective fiber would meet the center, so the projection is finite. The specified fibers are singletons; the fiber at \\(q_0\\) is reduced by injectivity of the differential and Nakayama’s lemma. The generic degree is therefore one. Pulling back general hyperplanes gives image degree \\(a\\).</p><p>For multiplicities, put\n\\[\nA=\\mathcal O_{\\pi(X),\\pi(p_i)},\\qquad B=\\mathcal O_{X,p_i}.\n\\]\nThen \\(B\\) is finite local over \\(A\\), of rank one, and \\(B/A\\) has dimension at most \\(k-1\\). Artin–Rees and the exact sequence for the \\(\\mathfrak m\\)-adic quotients show that \\(A/\\mathfrak m^j\\) and \\(B/\\mathfrak m^jB\\) have the same leading coefficient. Since \\(\\mathfrak mB\\subseteq\\mathfrak n\\),\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb C}B/\\mathfrak m^jB\\ge\n\\dim_{\\mathbb C}B/\\mathfrak n^j.\n\\]\nComparison of leading coefficients proves the multiplicity inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "Corollary \\(3.13\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(X\\subsetneq\\mathbb C^m\\) be irreducible of degree \\(a\\), and \\(S\\subset X\\) finite, with \\(\\operatorname{mult}_pX>a/2\\) for every \\(p\\in S\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(X\\subsetneq\\mathbb C^m\\) be irreducible of degree \\(a\\), and \\(S\\subset X\\) finite, with \\(\\operatorname{mult}_pX&gt;a/2\\) for every \\(p\\in S\\). For every specified \\(x_*\\notin X\\), there is a polynomial of degree at most \\(2(m+1)\\), vanishing on \\(S\\) and nonzero at \\(x_*\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Dimension zero is handled by a linear polynomial. Otherwise apply Lemma \\(3.3\\) to project to a hypersurface while preserving the specified points and excluding \\(x_*\\). Pull back its defining equation. It has degree at most \\(a\\), is nonzero at \\(x_*\\), and has order at least \\(\\operatorname{mult}_pX\\) at each \\(p\\). Apply Lemma \\(3.1\\) with \\(r=\\lfloor a/2\\rfloor+1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.5", "orig": "Corollary \\(3.14\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let an irreducible affine curve \\(C\\) lie in a variety \\(X\\) smooth at \\(p\\in C\\).", "statement": "<p>Let an irreducible affine curve \\(C\\) lie in a variety \\(X\\) smooth at \\(p\\in C\\). If some \\(z\\in C\\) satisfies \\(z-p\\notin T_pX\\), then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{mult}_pC\\le\\tfrac12\\deg C.\n\\]\nIf projection of \\(C\\) from \\(p\\) is nonconstant, its image degree times its generic map degree is at least \\(\\tfrac12\\deg C\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose an affine hyperplane containing \\(p+T_pX\\) but not \\(z\\), with equation \\(\\ell\\). Its restriction belongs to \\(\\mathfrak m_{X,p}^2\\), hence to \\(\\mathfrak m_{C,p}^2\\), and is nonzero on \\(C\\). The one-dimensional reduced local ring \\(R=\\mathcal O_{C,p}\\) is Cohen–Macaulay, so\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}R/(\\ell^j)\n=j\\,\\operatorname{length}R/(\\ell).\n\\]\nSince \\((\\ell^j)\\subseteq\\mathfrak m_{C,p}^{2j}\\), comparison of Hilbert–Samuel leading terms gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}R/(\\ell)\\ge2\\operatorname{mult}_pC.\n\\]\nBézout bounds this length by \\(\\deg C\\). Apply Lemma \\(3.3\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.6", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.15\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(C\\subset\\mathbb P^2_{\\mathbb C}\\) is a reduced plane curve of degree \\(a\\), and \\(m_q=\\operatorname{mult}_qC\\), then \\( \\sum_{m_q\\ge2}m_q(m_q-1)\\le a(a-1),\\qquad \\sum_{m_q\\ge2}m_q^2\\le2a(a-1). \\) The sums include …", "statement": "<p>If \\(C\\subset\\mathbb P^2_{\\mathbb C}\\) is a reduced plane curve of degree \\(a\\), and \\(m_q=\\operatorname{mult}_qC\\), then\n\\[\n\\sum_{m_q\\ge2}m_q(m_q-1)\\le a(a-1),\\qquad\n\\sum_{m_q\\ge2}m_q^2\\le2a(a-1).\n\\]\nThe sums include points at infinity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(C=Z(F)\\), with \\(F\\) homogeneous squarefree. A general first polar\n\\[\nG=\\sum_{i=0}^2\\lambda_i\\partial_{X_i}F\n\\]\nhas degree \\(a-1\\) and no common factor with \\(F\\). In a local affine chart, its equation has order at least \\(m_q-1\\). Thus\n\\[\nI_q(F,G)\\ge m_q(m_q-1).\n\\]\nThis follows by substituting into primitive local branch parametrizations and summing branch orders. Bézout gives the first inequality. The second follows from \\(m^2\\le2m(m-1)\\) for \\(m\\ge2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Theorem \\(B.5\\) and Corollary \\(B.6\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be \\(L\\) distinct real affine lines in \\(\\mathbb R^3\\), with at most \\(B_0\\) in any affine plane.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be \\(L\\) distinct real affine lines in \\(\\mathbb R^3\\), with at most \\(B_0\\) in any affine plane. For every integer \\(k\\ge3\\), the number \\(R_k\\) of points incident to at least \\(k\\) lines satisfies\n\\[\nR_k\\le C\\left(\\frac{L^{3/2}}{k^2}+\\frac{LB_0}{k^3}+\\frac Lk\\right).\n\\]\nNo regulus hypothesis is imposed.</p><p>Consequently, if \\(U,V\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) have sizes \\(u,v\\), then, for \\(3\\le k\\le\\min(u,v)\\), at most\n\\[\nC(uv)^{3/2}/k^2\n\\]\nEuclidean isometries \\(g\\), of both orientations, satisfy \\(|g(U)\\cap V|\\ge k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First take \\(s\\) points each incident to between \\(k\\) and \\(2k\\) lines, and suppose at least \\(L/100\\) lines each contain \\(sk/(100L)\\) of them. Unless\n\\[\ns\\le A(L^{3/2}k^{-2}+Lk^{-1}),\n\\]\nput \\(r=\\lfloor\\theta L^2s^{-1}k^{-3}\\rfloor\\) and apply Lemma \\(4.3\\). If a fixed positive proportion of the points lay outside \\(Z(f)\\), some sufficiently occupied cell would be met by at most \\(C_\\varepsilon L/r^2\\) lines. Lemma \\(4.2\\) inside that cell gives\n\\[\ns/r^3\\le C_\\varepsilon\\left(L^2/(r^4k^3)+L/(r^2k)\\right).\n\\]\nSubstitution gives\n\\[\ns\\le C_\\varepsilon(\\theta^{-1}s+\\theta L^3s^{-1}k^{-4}),\n\\]\ncontradicting the assumed lower bound after choosing \\(\\theta\\), then \\(A\\).</p><p>Replace \\(f\\) by its squarefree part. Its degree satisfies\n\\[\nD\\le C\\theta L^2s^{-1}k^{-3},\\qquad\nD/(sk/L)\\le C\\theta/A^2,\\qquad\nD/\\sqrt L\\le C\\theta/(Ak).\n\\]\nAll but \\(2\\varepsilon s\\) points lie on at least three lines contained in \\(Z(f)\\): otherwise count intersections with lines outside the zero set. Incidence counting then leaves at least \\(L/200\\) lines, each with at least \\(sk/(200L)\\) such points. Lemma \\(4.4\\) makes these lines critical or flat. Its exceptional-line bounds leave at least \\(L/400\\) in plane components. One plane contains at least \\(L/(400D)\\) lines, whence\n\\[\ns\\le CLB_0k^{-3}.\n\\]</p><p>Remove the uniformity hypothesis by induction on \\(L\\). If fewer than \\(L/100\\) lines meet the threshold \\(sk/(100L)\\), those lines contain at least \\(9k/10\\) incidences at at least \\(9s/10\\) points. Split into two multiplicity classes, apply induction with \\(k_1=k\\) or \\(k_1=\\lceil9k/10\\rceil\\), and use\n\\[\n(20/9)(1/100)(10/9)^3&lt;1.\n\\]\nFinally sum multiplicity buckets \\([2^jk,2^{j+1}k)\\).</p><p>For planar motions, separate orientations by reflection and rotate the second cloud generically so that every counted direct motion lies in a Cayley chart. With \\(J(x_1,x_2)=(-x_2,x_1)\\), a match \\(x\\mapsto y\\) corresponds to\n\\[\nc=(x-y)/2-tJ(x+y)/2.\n\\]\nThese \\(uv\\) lines are distinct. A containing plane \\(a\\cdot c+bt=d\\) gives\n\\[\na\\cdot(x-y)=2d,\\qquad a\\cdot J(x+y)=2b.\n\\]\nFor fixed either endpoint, these determine the other, so the plane cap is \\(\\min(u,v)\\). Apply the line theorem; its first term dominates the other two in the stated range.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(B.1\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Suppose a simple graph with \\(v\\ge1\\) vertices and \\(e\\ge4v\\) edges is drawn in the plane, no edge passes through another vertex, and edges sharing an endpoint have no other intersection.", "statement": "<p>Suppose a simple graph with \\(v\\ge1\\) vertices and \\(e\\ge4v\\) edges is drawn in the plane, no edge passes through another vertex, and edges sharing an endpoint have no other intersection. If \\(X\\) counts intersecting edge pairs away from endpoints, then\n\\[\nX\\ge e^3/(64v^2).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Deleting at most one edge per intersecting pair leaves a planar graph, so \\(e\\le3v+X\\). Retain each vertex independently with probability \\(p\\). Taking expectations yields\n\\[\np^2e\\le3pv+p^4X.\n\\]\nSet \\(p=4v/e\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Theorem \\(B.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(n\\) real planar points and \\(L\\) distinct lines, \\( I\\le C(n^{2/3}L^{2/3}+n+L). \\) The number of \\(k\\)-rich points is at most \\(C(L^2/k^3+L/k)\\), and the number of \\(k\\)-rich lines of an \\(n\\)-point set is at most …", "statement": "<p>For \\(n\\) real planar points and \\(L\\) distinct lines,\n\\[\nI\\le C(n^{2/3}L^{2/3}+n+L).\n\\]\nThe number of \\(k\\)-rich points is at most \\(C(L^2/k^3+L/k)\\), and the number of \\(k\\)-rich lines of an \\(n\\)-point set is at most \\(C(n^2/k^3+n/k)\\), for \\(k\\ge2\\). These estimates also hold for finite real affine or projective configurations in fixed dimension.</p>", "proof": "<p>Join consecutive sample points on each line. The resulting graph is simple, has \\(e\\ge I-L\\), and at most \\(\\binom L2\\) crossing pairs. Lemma \\(4.1\\) proves the incidence bound. For rich points, use \\(ks\\le C(s^{2/3}L^{2/3}+s+L)\\), absorbing the linear term for large \\(k\\); bounded \\(k\\) is handled by pairs of lines. Duality gives rich lines. Generic planar projection preserves distinct specified points and lines and all existing incidences. For projective data, first choose a generic affine chart.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(B.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a finite \\(S\\subset\\mathbb R^3\\) and integer \\(r\\ge1\\), there is a nonzero real polynomial \\(f\\), of degree at most \\(Cr\\), such that \\(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus Z(f)\\) is partitioned into at most \\(Cr^3\\) open sets …", "statement": "<p>For a finite \\(S\\subset\\mathbb R^3\\) and integer \\(r\\ge1\\), there is a nonzero real polynomial \\(f\\), of degree at most \\(Cr\\), such that \\(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus Z(f)\\) is partitioned into at most \\(Cr^3\\) open sets, each containing at most \\(C|S|/r^3\\) sample points. A line not contained in \\(Z(f)\\) meets at most \\(\\deg f+1\\) of these sets. The sets need not be connected.</p>", "proof": "<p>Borsuk–Ulam applied to signed-volume imbalances supplies a degree-\\(D\\) polynomial bisecting fewer than \\(\\binom{D+3}{3}\\) bounded measurable sets. Continuity follows from dominated convergence and the measure-zero property of nonzero polynomial zero sets. Replace each sample point by a small ball and pass to a convergent normalized coefficient sequence to obtain finite-set bisection.</p><p>Iterate \\(J\\) times, bisecting the at most \\(2^{j-1}\\) sign classes at stage \\(j\\), with degree \\(C2^{j/3}\\). Their product has degree \\(C2^{J/3}\\). Choose \\(2^J\\) comparable to \\(r^3\\). On a line outside the product zero set, all factor signs are constant on each of the at most \\(\\deg f+1\\) remaining intervals.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "Lemma \\(B.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a squarefree real polynomial \\(f\\) of degree \\(D\\), a point of \\(Z(f)\\) is critical if \\(\\nabla f=0\\), and a regular point is flat if its second fundamental form vanishes.", "statement": "<p>For a squarefree real polynomial \\(f\\) of degree \\(D\\), a point of \\(Z(f)\\) is critical if \\(\\nabla f=0\\), and a regular point is flat if its second fundamental form vanishes. A contained line is critical if all its points are critical, and flat if it is not critical and all its regular points are flat.</p><p>A point on three distinct contained lines is critical or flat. There are at most \\(D(D-1)\\) critical lines. A line with more than \\(D\\) critical points is critical; a line with more than \\(3D\\) flat points is flat. At most \\(3D^2\\) flat lines fail to lie in plane components.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a regular point, the contained line directions lie in the tangent plane and annihilate the quadratic second fundamental form. Three distinct directions force that quadratic to vanish.</p><p>A generic linear combination of first derivatives shares no factor with squarefree \\(f\\); Bézout bounds critical lines. Restriction to a line proves the critical-point assertion.</p><p>With \\(H_f\\) the Hessian, the nine scalar components of\n\\[\nT_j=\\bigl(H_f(e_j\\times\\nabla f)\\bigr)\\times\\nabla f\n\\]\nhave degree at most \\(3D\\) and test flatness at regular points. Restriction proves the flat-line assertion. If all these tests vanished on a nonlinear irreducible factor having a regular real point, its normal would be locally constant, giving an open planar piece and hence a linear factor. Therefore some test is not divisible by that factor; Bézout bounds its flat lines by \\(3D\\) times its degree. Sum over nonplanar factors. A flat line belongs to only one factor, since membership in two makes it critical.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Theorem \\(B.12\\) and Corollary \\(B.13\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every \\(n\\ge2\\) points \\(Q\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) determine at least \\( cn/\\log(2n) \\) distinct positive distances.", "statement": "<p>Every \\(n\\ge2\\) points \\(Q\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) determine at least\n\\[\ncn/\\log(2n)\n\\]\ndistinct positive distances.</p><p>Consequently, if a finite Euclidean point set determines at most \\(M\\ge1\\) positive distances, each affine two-plane contains at most\n\\[\nCM\\log(2M)\n\\]\nof its points.</p>", "proof": "<p>Rotate a second copy \\(Q'\\) of \\(Q\\) generically so that all direct isometries with at least two matches lie in the Cayley chart. Use the \\(n^2\\) pair lines. Lemmas \\(5.4\\) and \\(5.5\\) bound their two-rich points by \\(Cn^3\\), while Proposition \\(4\\) bounds \\(k\\)-rich points by \\(Cn^3/k^2\\) for \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).</p><p>Let \\(a_\\rho\\) count ordered nonzero segments of length \\(\\rho\\) in \\(Q\\). Two equally long ordered segments determine a unique direct planar isometry. Therefore\n\\[\n\\sum_\\rho a_\\rho^2=\\sum_gk_g(k_g-1)\\le Cn^3\\log(2n),\n\\]\nby dyadic summation. Since \\(\\sum_\\rho a_\\rho=n(n-1)\\), Cauchy–Schwarz proves the distance bound.</p><p>For plane occupancy \\(n\\), this gives \\(n\\le CM\\log(2n)\\). First use \\(\\log(2n)\\le C\\sqrt n\\) to obtain \\(n=O(M^2)\\), and substitute back to get \\(n\\le CM\\log(2M)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(B.7\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "An irreducible degree-\\(d\\) surface in \\(\\mathbb C^3\\) that is not geometrically ruled contains at most \\(17d^2\\) affine lines.", "statement": "<p>An irreducible degree-\\(d\\) surface in \\(\\mathbb C^3\\) that is not geometrically ruled contains at most \\(17d^2\\) affine lines.</p>", "proof": "<p>Planes and irreducible projective quadrics are ruled, so assume \\(d\\ge3\\). Let \\(F\\) be irreducible, and choose coordinates with \\(A=F_z\\not\\equiv0\\); put \\(B=F_x\\), \\(C=F_y\\). For\n\\[\nV(u,v)=(Au,Av,-Bu-Cv),\n\\]\nform the binary quadratic and cubic\n\\[\nq=D^2F[V,V],\\qquad c=D^3F[V,V,V].\n\\]\nTheir coefficient degrees are at most \\(3d-4\\) and \\(4d-6\\). Their resultant \\(R\\) has degree at most \\(17d-24\\). Every contained line lies in \\(Z(R)\\); where \\(A=0\\), the forms are\n\\[\nq=F_{zz}(Bu+Cv)^2,\\qquad c=-F_{zzz}(Bu+Cv)^3,\n\\]\nso the assertion also holds there.</p><p>If \\(F\\mid R\\), work over an algebraic closure of \\(\\mathbb C(Z(F))\\), extending commuting \\(\\partial_x,\\partial_y\\), and regard \\(z\\) as algebraic over \\(\\mathbb C(x,y)\\). Put\n\\[\nr=z_{xx},\\quad s=z_{xy},\\quad t=z_{yy}.\n\\]\nIf the Hessian is zero, a coordinate derivation already gives a line. Otherwise change \\(x,y\\) so \\(t\\ne0\\). A finite common tangent slope \\(a\\) satisfies\n\\[\nQ(a)=r+2sa+ta^2=0,\n\\]\n\\[\nP(a)=z_{xxx}+3az_{xxy}+3a^2z_{xyy}+a^3z_{yyy}=0.\n\\]\nFor \\(D=\\partial_x+a\\partial_y\\), differentiation gives \\(Da=0\\) when the root is simple. For a repeated root, use \\(s=-ta\\), \\(r=ta^2\\), and mixed-partial identities to obtain the same conclusion. Thus\n\\[\nD^2x=D^2y=D^2z=0.\n\\]\nThe formal Taylor homomorphism gives\n\\[\nF(x+T,y+aT,z+(z_x+az_y)T)=0.\n\\]\nThe geometric generic point lies on a contained line. The projective point–line incidence has closed image, so the whole surface is ruled. Therefore, in the nonruled case, \\(F\\) and \\(R\\) are coprime, and Bézout proves the bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(B.8\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "An irreducible ruled projective surface over \\(\\mathbb C\\), neither a plane nor a smooth quadric, has a unique irreducible one-dimensional generator family covering it.", "statement": "<p>An irreducible ruled projective surface over \\(\\mathbb C\\), neither a plane nor a smooth quadric, has a unique irreducible one-dimensional generator family covering it. A generic point belongs to exactly one generator. At most one point lies on infinitely many contained lines. At most two contained lines meet infinitely many others away from such points; every line outside the generator family is one of these.</p>", "proof": "<p>A two-dimensional family of lines would give infinitely many lines through a generic smooth point, filling its tangent plane and making the surface planar. Every curve component of the line variety covers the surface, since its projective incidence image is closed and infinitely many lines cannot lie in a proper closed subset.</p><p>If a generic point had two generators, irreducibility and closed meeting loci would yield two curve families whose every pair of members meets. Three pairwise skew members of one family determine a smooth quadric, whose common transversals form the opposite ruling, forcing the surface to be that quadric. Otherwise the family is pairwise meeting and hence coplanar or concurrent. The coplanar case is a plane; the concurrent case is a cone, whose generic point has a unique generator. This proves generic uniqueness and uniqueness of the family.</p><p>An exceptional point lies on every generator, so there is at most one. An exceptional line meets every generator. Three pairwise skew exceptional lines force a quadric. Two exceptional lines cannot meet: irreducibility would put all generators in their plane or through their intersection, contradicting their defining property away from exceptional points. Every isolated contained line outside the family is met at distinct points by infinitely many generators, hence is exceptional.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(B.9\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let a union of real absolutely irreducible geometrically ruled surfaces have total degree \\(d\\ge1\\).", "statement": "<p>Let a union of real absolutely irreducible geometrically ruled surfaces have total degree \\(d\\ge1\\). Let \\(M\\) distinct real affine lines be contained in it, with at most \\(s\\) in any plane or regulus. Their two-rich points number at most\n\\[\n5dM+sM/2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First exclude plane and smooth-quadric components. There are at most \\(d\\) exceptional points and \\(2d\\) exceptional lines by Lemma \\(5.2\\); restore their contributions at cost at most \\(d+2dM\\).</p><p>For a remaining line \\(\\ell\\), intersect the squarefree surface equation with a generic plane through \\(\\ell\\), and divide out the entire power of its linear factor. The residual curve has degree at most \\(d-1\\) and does not contain \\(\\ell\\). Every remaining intersection \\(\\ell\\cap\\ell'\\) belongs to this curve: vary the generator \\(\\ell'\\), use closedness of the residual curve, and note that an infinite family meeting \\(\\ell\\) would make \\(\\ell\\) or the intersection point exceptional. Bézout gives at most \\(d-1\\) such points on \\(\\ell\\), yielding at most \\(4dM\\) in total.</p><p>Extract plane and smooth-quadric groups one at a time. Each has at most \\(s\\) lines and contributes at most \\(s\\) times its size divided by two internally. Lines outside an extracted component meet it at at most its degree points, costing at most \\(dM\\) over all extractions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "Theorem \\(B.10\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(m\\) distinct real affine lines, with at most \\(s\\ge1\\) in any plane or regulus, the number \\(P_2\\) of two-rich points satisfies \\( P_2\\le K(m^{3/2}+ms). \\)", "statement": "<p>For \\(m\\) distinct real affine lines, with at most \\(s\\ge1\\) in any plane or regulus, the number \\(P_2\\) of two-rich points satisfies\n\\[\nP_2\\le K(m^{3/2}+ms).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on \\(m\\), keeping \\(s\\). Write \\(H(m,s)=m^{3/2}+ms\\), and suppose \\(P_2&gt;KH(m,s)\\). Delete lines with fewer than\n\\[\nT=(K/4)(\\sqrt m+s)\n\\]\ndistinct remaining intersections. More than \\(3KH(m,s)/4\\) two-rich points remain.</p><p>Sample remaining lines with probability \\(64/K\\). For each fixed line, choose distinct witness lines at its distinct intersection points. Chernoff gives probability at most \\(e^{-2\\sqrt m}\\) of fewer than \\(8\\sqrt m\\) sampled witnesses. A union bound and Markov’s inequality give a sample of \\(q\\le128m/K\\) lines with every remaining line meeting it in at least \\(8\\sqrt m\\) points.</p><p>A nonzero polynomial of degree \\(D=\\lceil\\sqrt{6q}\\rceil&lt;4\\sqrt q\\) vanishes on the sample by coefficient counting. Since \\(D&lt;8\\sqrt m\\), it vanishes on every remaining line. Replace it by its squarefree part. Lemma \\(5.1\\), and Bézout for nonreal conjugate factors, leave at most\n\\[\nb\\le18D^2\\le36864m/K\\le m/4\n\\]\nlines outside the real absolutely irreducible ruled factors. Those lines meet the ruled union in at most \\(bD\\) points. Lemma \\(5.3\\) and induction bound the remaining two-rich points by\n\\[\n6Dm+sm/2+KH(b,s)\n\\le(49+K/4)H(m,s),\n\\]\ncontradicting the lower bound for sufficiently large \\(K\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.5", "orig": "Lemma \\(B.11\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For planar sets \\(U,V\\) of sizes \\(u,v\\), the \\(uv\\) Cayley lines \\( c=(x-y)/2-tJ(x+y)/2 \\) have at most \\(\\min(u,v)\\) members in any plane and at most \\(6u+2v\\) in any regulus.", "statement": "<p>For planar sets \\(U,V\\) of sizes \\(u,v\\), the \\(uv\\) Cayley lines\n\\[\nc=(x-y)/2-tJ(x+y)/2\n\\]\nhave at most \\(\\min(u,v)\\) members in any plane and at most \\(6u+2v\\) in any regulus.</p>", "proof": "<p>The plane bound was proved in Proposition \\(4\\). Fix \\(x\\) and allow \\(y\\in\\mathbb R^2\\). These lines partition parameter space and are pairwise projectively skew. The nowhere-zero polynomial vector field\n\\[\nW_x(t,c)=\\bigl(1+t^2,(tI+J)(c-x)\\bigr)\n\\]\nis tangent to them.</p><p>For a regulus \\(Z(f)\\), seven fixed-\\(x\\) lines force \\(f\\mid W_x\\cdot\\nabla f\\), since the latter degree is at most three. Write \\(W_x\\cdot\\nabla f=hf\\). Restriction to a fixed-\\(x\\) line gives\n\\[\n(1+t^2)g'(t)=h(t,c(t))g(t).\n\\]\nA nonzero polynomial solution cannot have a real root, so every such line through a real point of the regulus is contained in it. Thus the fixed-\\(x\\) family supplies a full real affine ruling. Different fixed-\\(x\\) families share no line, and there are two rulings. At most two \\(x\\)'s contribute seven or more lines; these contribute at most \\(2v\\), and the rest at most \\(6u\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix \\(d\\ge3\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(d\\ge3\\). Assume the claimed constant-factor distance bound is available in dimensions \\(3\\le u&lt;d\\). Let \\(P\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\) vary along a sequence with\n\\[\nN=|P|\\to\\infty,\\quad\nM=1+|\\Delta(P)|,\\quad\nB=N^{1/d},\\quad A=B^2,\\quad M/A\\to0.\n\\]\nThen an affine \\(u\\)-flat contains at most\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n1,&amp;u=0,\\\\\n2M,&amp;u=1,\\\\\nCM\\log(2M),&amp;u=2,\\\\\nC_uM^{u/2},&amp;3\\le u&lt;d\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nsample points. A circle contains at most \\(2M\\).</p><p>For some fixed \\(c_0&gt;0\\), every proper real or complex affine flat contains at most \\(NB^{-c_0}\\) real sample points, eventually. Planes have the sharper bound \\(B^{2+o(1)}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>From a sample point on a line, each positive distance is realized at most twice on that line. The same argument on a circle uses its intersections with a prescribed-radius circle. Proposition \\(5\\) gives the planar bound. Apply the available lower-dimensional theorem in every higher proper flat.</p><p>Eventually \\(M\\le B^2\\), so the proper-flat bounds are \\(O(B^2\\log B)\\) in dimension two and \\(O(B^u)\\) for \\(u\\ge3\\). Thus \\(c_0=1/2\\), for example, works eventually. The real affine span of the real points of a complex flat has real dimension at most its complex dimension, giving the complex assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Work in the setting of Proposition \\(6\\), and write \\(\\pi_x(y)=[y-x]\\).", "statement": "<p>Work in the setting of Proposition \\(6\\), and write \\(\\pi_x(y)=[y-x]\\). Let \\(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal Y\\) be finite families of distinct irreducible carriers of dimensions \\(k\\ge l\\ge1\\), with \\(k&lt;d\\), \\(l&lt;d-1\\). Give them disjoint assigned subsets \\(S_X,T_Y\\subset P\\), separately within each family, of total masses \\(n_x,n_y\\ge N\\exp(-o(w))\\), where \\(w\\to\\infty\\), \\(w\\le\\log B\\). Pool repeated carriers. Require all flat members to be real.</p><p>When \\(k=l\\), suppose the number of flat members in each family is at most \\(e\\exp(o(w))\\), and each normalized assigned weight is at most \\(e^{-1}\\exp(o(w))\\).</p><p>For every fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), discard component pairs of total normalized product weight \\(o(1)\\). For each retained source \\(X\\), the remaining target list \\(\\mathcal Y_X\\) then has the following property: at a general pair in each \\(X\\times Y\\), both tangent spaces project injectively, and the number of moving roots on \\(\\bigcup_{Z\\in\\mathcal Y_X}Z\\) over \\(\\pi_x(y)\\), including generic map degrees but excluding the undefined root \\(x\\), is at most \\(\\exp(\\varepsilon w)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Discard generic tangencies. For fixed general \\(y\\in Y\\), any source component producing tangency at \\(y\\) lies in \\(y+T_yY\\), and its total assigned mass is at most \\(NB^{-c_0}\\). Normalize and average. Interchanging factors removes tangencies at the source.</p><p>When \\(k=l\\), also discard pairs in a common \\((k+1)\\)-flat with a nonlinear member. That member spans the flat, so fixing it pins down a proper flat containing the other assigned points; weighted averaging again gives \\(o(1)\\).</p><p>Lemma \\(7.1\\) leaves multiple moving roots only when \\(k=l\\) and the relevant components are flats in a common \\((k+1)\\)-flat \\(H\\). Every further target with that projection also lies in \\(H\\), is flat, and contributes one root.</p><p>Identify flats occurring in both families, adding their weights. Remove weights below \\(e^{-1}\\exp(-\\eta w)\\), losing \\(\\exp(-\\eta w+o(w))\\). Slice by a generic real projective subspace of codimension \\(k\\). Flats become points and generated \\((k+1)\\)-flats become distinct lines, with exactly the required incidences. Their number is at most \\(e\\exp(o(w))\\). If \\(t_H\\) flats lie in \\(H\\), the weight cutoff and proper-flat cap give\n\\[\nt_H\\le e\\exp(\\eta w+o(w))B^{-c_0}\\le eB^{-c_0/2},\n\\]\nfor \\(\\eta&lt;c_0/4\\).</p><p>Lemma \\(4.2\\) bounds dyadic \\(t\\)-rich lines by\n\\[\nO(e^2\\exp(o(w))/t^3+e\\exp(o(w))/t).\n\\]\nEach has pair weight at most \\(t^2e^{-2}\\exp(o(w))\\). Summing \\(t\\ge\\exp(\\varepsilon w)\\) gives\n\\[\n\\exp(-\\varepsilon w+o(w))+B^{-c_0/2}\\exp(o(w))=o(1).\n\\]\nDelete these pairs. The remaining target lists have the asserted depth.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.1\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(X,Y\\subset\\mathbb A^d\\) be irreducible of dimensions \\(k,l\\ge1\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(X,Y\\subset\\mathbb A^d\\) be irreducible of dimensions \\(k,l\\ge1\\). Suppose the general connecting direction lies in neither endpoint tangent space. If a general connecting line has a further point on an irreducible \\(l\\)-fold \\(Y'\\), then \\(k\\le l\\). If \\(k=l\\), \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) lie in a common affine \\((k+1)\\)-flat. Equality of the projected tangent spaces at a general pair already gives that conclusion.</p><p>Locally, suppose \\(p\\in X,q\\in Y\\) are smooth and\n\\[\n\\psi(x,y)=(x,\\pi_x(y))\n\\]\nhas differential rank \\(k+l\\) at \\((p,q)\\). Suppose one smooth local \\((k+l)\\)-fold at \\((p,\\pi_pq)\\) contains both product images from the same source \\(X\\), paired with \\(Y\\) and \\(Y'\\). If \\(z\\in Y'\\setminus\\{p,q\\}\\) has \\(\\pi_pz=\\pi_pq\\), then\n\\[\n\\overline{T_pX}\\subseteq\\overline{T_qY}\n\\quad\\text{in }\\mathbb C^d/\\mathbb C(q-p).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>In a direction chart write \\(y-x=au\\), \\(z-x=bu\\), with distinct nonzero \\(a,b\\). Source velocity \\(\\xi\\) with target fixed gives direction velocities \\(-\\bar\\xi/a\\), \\(-\\bar\\xi/b\\). Their difference is vertical and equals \\((b^{-1}-a^{-1})\\bar\\xi\\).</p><p>The first image has dimension \\(k+l\\), with vertical tangent kernel \\(\\overline{T_yY}\\). If a further root exists generally, the two irreducible images coincide, and the subtraction gives\n\\[\n\\overline{T_xX}\\subseteq\\overline{T_yY}.\n\\]\nThe identical calculation proves the local assertion using the specified smooth local containing variety.</p><p>For \\(k=l\\), the projected tangents agree. Fix general \\(y\\) and project \\(X\\) away from \\(y+T_yY\\). Its differential is zero. In characteristic zero its irreducible image is a point, so \\(X\\) lies in an affine \\((k+1)\\)-flat containing that tangent plane. Interchange \\(X,Y\\). If either affine span has dimension \\(k+1\\), it is the common flat; if both have dimension \\(k\\), the tangent inclusions bound their affine join by \\(k+1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition \\(4.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Work in the setting of Proposition \\(6\\).", "statement": "<p>Work in the setting of Proposition \\(6\\). For every \\(p\\in P\\), let \\(C_p\\subset\\mathbb P^{d-1}\\) be a union of irreducible curves, each spanning projective dimension at most two, of total degree at most\n\\[\nE=\\delta N/A,\\qquad\\delta\\to0.\n\\]\nAssign sample points partially and without repetition to irreducible physical curves \\(Y_i\\), pooling repeated curves and requiring line members to be real. Then the number of ordered pairs \\(p\\ne q\\), with \\(q\\) assigned to \\(Y_i\\) and\n\\[\n\\overline{\\pi_pY_i}\\subseteq C_p,\n\\]\nis \\(o(N^2)\\), uniformly in the assignment and curves.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(n_i\\) for assigned mass and \\(d_i=\\deg Y_i\\). Lemma \\(1.1\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sum_i n_i\\le N,\\qquad n_i\\le CMd_i.\n\\]</p><p>For assigned lines, centers on the line cost at most \\(2M\\sum_i n_i=o(N^2)\\). Otherwise the projected line is a line component of \\(C_p\\), corresponding to a plane \\(H\\). Planes with \\(S_H&lt;A\\) cost at most \\(NAE=\\delta N^2\\); the others are handled by Lemma \\(8.1\\).</p><p>For \\(d\\ge4\\), nonlinear curves with three-dimensional affine span require centers in their fixed three-flat; planar curves viewed from their own plane are also negligible by the flat cap. Outside those planes projection preserves degree. Group equal nonlinear projections, of degree \\(e_\\gamma\\), and let their mass be \\(W_{p,\\gamma}\\). Groups with \\(W_{p,\\gamma}\\le KMe_\\gamma\\) cost \\(O(NME)=o(N^2)\\). In larger groups, off-diagonal weight is at least \\(W_{p,\\gamma}^2/2\\). If their total pair mass were at least \\(\\varepsilon N^2\\), weighted Cauchy–Schwarz would give\n\\[\n\\sum_{p,\\gamma}e_\\gamma^{-1}\\sum_{i\\ne j}n_in_j\n\\ge\\varepsilon^2N^3/(2E).\n\\]\nLemma \\(8.2\\) bounds this by \\(CN^2\\), impossible because \\(E=o(N)\\).</p><p>For \\(d=3\\), make the deletions of Lemma \\(8.3\\), costing \\(O(N)+o(N^2)\\). Again discard groups of mass at most \\(KMe_\\gamma\\). An invalid triple repeats an index with \\(t_i\\le2\\), whose mass is at most \\(4CMe_\\gamma\\). Its total weight is at most\n\\[\n3W_{p,\\gamma}\\sum_{t_i\\le2}n_i^2\n\\le12CMe_\\gamma W_{p,\\gamma}^2.\n\\]\nFor large fixed \\(K\\), valid triples carry at least half the cubic weight. Hölder would turn surviving pair mass \\(\\varepsilon N^2\\) into\n\\[\n\\sum_{p,\\gamma}e_\\gamma^{-2}\n\\sum_{(i,j,k)\\text{ valid}}n_in_jn_k\n\\ge\\varepsilon^3N^4/(2E^2).\n\\]\nLemma \\(8.3\\) bounds this by \\(CN^3\\). Since \\(E=\\delta N^{1/3}\\), this is impossible.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Within the setting of Proposition \\(8\\), let \\(n_i\\) be the assigned mass of a line \\(Y_i\\).", "statement": "<p>Within the setting of Proposition \\(8\\), let \\(n_i\\) be the assigned mass of a line \\(Y_i\\). For a plane \\(H\\), put\n\\[\nS_H=\\sum_{Y_i\\subset H,\\ Y_i\\text{ a line}}n_i,\\qquad h_H=|P\\cap H|.\n\\]\nIf an assigned line in \\(H\\) has weight \\(b\\), then\n\\[\nbh_H\\le CM^2.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\sum_{H:S_H\\ge A}h_HS_H=o(N^2).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put the line on the first coordinate axis in \\(H\\), and map\n\\[\n(x_1,x_2)\\mapsto(x_1,x_1^2+x_2^2).\n\\]\nFibers have size at most two. Distances from an axis point \\((a,0)\\) give lines\n\\[\nv-2au=r^2-a^2.\n\\]\nThere are at most \\(bM\\) such lines and at least \\(bh_H/2\\) incidences. Lemma \\(4.2\\) yields\n\\[\nbh_H\\le C((bh_HM)^{2/3}+h_H+bM).\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(b\\), absorb \\(h_H\\); if \\(h_H=O(M)\\), use \\(b\\le2M\\), and otherwise absorb \\(bM\\). For bounded \\(b\\), two sample centers in \\(H\\) give \\(h_H\\le2M^2\\) by intersections of circles.</p><p>Bucket planes by \\(h_H\\asymp h\\), \\(S_H\\asymp s\\), with \\(h,s\\gtrsim A\\). Let \\(N_b\\) be total assigned mass on lines of dyadic weight \\(b\\). Only \\(b\\le L=CM^2/h\\) occur. A generic hyperplane slice and planar projection permit the weighted incidence estimate\n\\[\nFs\\le CF^{2/3}T(L)+CN+CLF,\\qquad\nT(L)=\\sum_{b\\le L}b^{1/3}N_b^{2/3},\n\\]\nwhere \\(F\\) is the number of planes in the bucket. Since \\(L/s=o(1)\\),\n\\[\nF\\le C(N/s+T(L)^3/s^3).\n\\]\nThe first contribution to \\(\\sum hsF\\) is\n\\[\nO(NB^{2+o(1)}(1+\\log N)^2)=o(N^2).\n\\]\nHölder gives\n\\[\nT(L)^3\\le CL\\sum_{b\\le L}(b/L)^{1/2}N_b^2.\n\\]\nSum geometrically over inverse dyadic \\(L\\ge b\\), then use\n\\[\n\\sum_bN_b^2\\le N^2,\\qquad \\sum_{s\\gtrsim A}s^{-2}=O(A^{-2}).\n\\]\nThe result is \\(O(N^2M^2/A^2)=o(N^2)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition \\(8\\), suppose \\(d\\ge4\\).", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition \\(8\\), suppose \\(d\\ge4\\). For two distinct planar nonlinear irreducible curves having the same nonlinear radial projection \\(\\gamma\\) from a center outside their planes, there are at most \\(C\\deg\\gamma\\) possible centers.</p>", "proof": "<p>The two planes and center lie in a projective three-space and meet in a line \\(L\\). Each perspective fixes \\(L\\) pointwise. Comparing perspectives gives a projectivity preserving the first curve and fixing \\(L\\) pointwise. With \\(L\\) at infinity it has form \\(u\\mapsto\\lambda u+b\\).</p><p>A nonidentity translation cannot preserve an irreducible nonlinear curve, since its infinite orbit is Zariski dense in a line. Thus the scalar homomorphism has trivial kernel, and commutators show the stabilizer is abelian. A nonidentity element has a unique affine fixed point, fixed by all commuting elements. The group consists of homotheties about that point. A generic free orbit lies on a line, so Bézout bounds its order by the curve degree and also excludes an infinite group. A perspective between distinct planes determines its center uniquely.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition \\(8\\), suppose \\(d=3\\).", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition \\(8\\), suppose \\(d=3\\). Remove centers satisfying \\(\\operatorname{mult}_pY_i&gt;\\deg Y_i/2\\), and projections to lines. At each remaining center, group nonlinear curves by their common projection \\(\\gamma\\), and write \\(t_i\\) for the generic projection degree.</p><p>An ordered triple \\((i,j,k)\\) is valid if each index occurs at most its corresponding \\(t_i\\). For every fixed ordered triple,\n\\[\n\\sum_{p:(i,j,k)\\text{ valid at }p}(\\deg\\gamma)^{-2}\\le C.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The high-multiplicity deletion has only boundedly many centers per curve, by a generic birational plane projection and Lemma \\(3.6\\). Projection to a line requires the curve and center to lie in a fixed plane. At retained centers,\n\\[\nd_i-\\operatorname{mult}_pY_i=t_i\\deg\\gamma,\\qquad\nd_i\\le2t_i\\deg\\gamma.\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(v\\) general on \\(Y_i\\), simultaneously for the finite center list, avoiding all lines joining pairs of centers. Those centers have distinct directions from \\(v\\). Viewed from \\(v\\), the targets \\(Y_j,Y_k\\) project birationally, with distinct images unless they coincide. Otherwise Lemma \\(7.1\\) puts the source and first target in one plane. Distinct curves in the same original nonlinear projection group cannot be coplanar: projection inside their plane has line image, and projection outside is injective. If the repeated source curve is planar, validity requires map degree at least two, again impossible in a nonlinear group.</p><p>The line \\(pv\\) gives at least\n\\[\nr_j=t_j-\\mathbf1_{i=j},\\qquad r_k=t_k-\\mathbf1_{i=k}\n\\]\ndistinct preimages other than \\(v\\). They are generic smooth points and give distinct normalization branches of the plane images. Validity gives\n\\[\nr_j\\ge t_j/2\\ge d_j/(4\\deg\\gamma),\\qquad\nr_k\\ge d_k/(4\\deg\\gamma).\n\\]\nFor distinct images, sum local multiplicity products by Bézout. For equal images, the residual multiplicity is at least two, and apply Lemma \\(3.6\\). Both give the displayed bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.4\\), with the scale-profile constructions of Propositions \\(5.1\\)–\\(5.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Work in the setting of Proposition \\(6\\), with an additional \\(\\delta\\to0\\) and \\(E=\\delta N/A\\).", "statement": "<p>Work in the setting of Proposition \\(6\\), with an additional \\(\\delta\\to0\\) and \\(E=\\delta N/A\\). For a window width \\(s\\to\\infty\\), \\(s\\le\\log B\\), define\n\\[\nT_s(b)=B\\exp(bs).\n\\]\nThe pieces, cut paths, and carrier families constructed in Lemmas \\(9.1\\)–\\(9.3\\) have the following product-testing property.</p><p>Take two pieces, or the permitted pools, of common width and limiting profile. On a fixed plateau assign each point to one containing carrier of its original piece before making tests. A polynomial of degree \\(O(T_s(b))\\), at a fixed interior time \\(b\\), vanishes at only \\(o(n_xn_y)\\) pairs on whose assigned product \\(X\\times Y\\) it is not identically zero.</p><p>The same holds for a bounded number of rational tests with nonzero denominators at the actual pairs and cleared numerator degrees \\(O(T_s(b))\\), uniformly bounded.</p><p>Consequently, an equation system containing labels of a dense graph contains the complete assigned component images at all but \\(o(n_xn_y)\\) graph pairs, whenever all pulled-back cleared numerators obey this common degree bound. Generically nonzero rational rank tests are nonzero at all but a negligible fraction of corresponding actual pairs under the same hypotheses.</p>", "proof": "<p>For each actual source \\(p\\), apply Lemma \\(9.3\\) to target carriers on which the specialized polynomial is nonzero. For the remaining case, fix a target carrier \\(Y\\). If the polynomial is nonzero on \\(X\\times Y\\), choose general \\(y\\in Y\\) making its specialization proper on all such \\(X\\), and apply the source version. Average with the disjoint assigned target masses.</p><p>For systems, clear denominators separately and take a generic linear combination of numerators, nonzero on every relevant product not contained in the system. A finite basis suffices regardless of the number of equations. For rank conditions, similarly combine the relevant minors so its zero set contains every rank failure. A bounded number of tests and a slightly later interior time absorb all fixed degree factors.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Proposition \\(5.1\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is \\(s_*\\to\\infty\\) and a partition of all but \\(o(N)\\) sample points into a core and non-core pieces.", "statement": "<p>There is \\(s_*\\to\\infty\\) and a partition of all but \\(o(N)\\) sample points into a core and non-core pieces.</p><p>The core is simultaneously isolated by equations of degree \\(L\\) with \\(EL^2=o(N)\\).</p><p>Every non-core piece has dyadic width \\(s=2^{-a}\\log B\\ge s_*\\), size \\(n=N\\exp(-o(s))\\), and one path of \\(d\\) proper hypersurface cuts. Along every sequence of pieces, after a subsequence their normalized testing times converge to\n\\[\n-1\\le b_1\\le\\cdots\\le b_d\\le1,\\qquad \\max_i|b_i|\\ge\\tfrac12.\n\\]\nThe \\(i\\)-th cut has degree at most \\(D_i\\exp(o(s))\\), where \\(D_i=B\\exp(b_is)\\).</p><p>On an open plateau, after \\(j\\) cuts, the local systems are pure of dimension \\(d-j\\). Uniformly over degree \\(O(T_s(b))\\) polynomials, the points at which a polynomial vanishes properly on every local carrier form \\(o(n)\\). This also holds before the first cut whenever \\(T_s(b)\\ge1\\), and is automatic after the last.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(s_*\\le\\frac14\\log B\\) slowly enough that \\(\\delta\\exp(6s_*)\\to0\\). Begin at width \\(\\log B\\), halve widths, and stop at the first in \\([s_*,2s_*]\\). On entry to width \\(s\\), the unresolved set is isolated at degree \\(B\\exp((1+o(1))s)\\), initially by Lemma \\(1.1\\). Except initially, no degree at most \\(B\\exp(-s)\\) polynomial captures \\(N\\exp(-\\sqrt{2s})\\) unresolved points.</p><p>At each nonfinal width, greedily remove batches of at least \\(N\\exp(-\\sqrt s)\\) on nonzero equations of degree at most \\(B\\exp(-s/2)\\), sending them to regularization. Then remove batches of that size isolated by systems of degree at most \\(B\\exp(s/2)\\); only their union proceeds to the next width. Send the remainder to regularization if it has at least \\(N\\exp(-s^{1/3})\\) points, otherwise discard it.</p><p>There are at most \\(\\exp(\\sqrt s)\\) batches of each kind. Lemma \\(1.3\\) isolates each second-kind batch exactly at degree \\(O(B\\exp(s/2))\\). Products of equations isolate their union at degree \\(B\\exp(s/2+O(\\sqrt s))\\). This preserves the entry invariant, and the first removal gives the next lower boundary. The final core has isolation degree at most \\(B\\exp(3s_*)\\), so \\(EL^2=o(N)\\).</p><p>For regularization, use a normalized-time grid with mesh tending to zero and \\(G=O(1+\\sqrt{\\log s})\\) points. Order potential steps by increasing time and decreasing positive dimension, giving \\(K=O_d(G)\\) indices. Set\n\\[\n\\lambda_i=e^{-a_i},\\qquad a_K=s^{1/10},\\qquad a_i=10a_{i+1}.\n\\]\nAt a node of original mass \\(m\\), repeatedly extract at least \\(\\lambda_im\\) remaining points cut properly by a polynomial of the testing degree. Each child lowers dimension and is processed at that time. The residual advances, unless smaller than \\(\\sqrt{\\lambda_i}m\\), when it is discarded. At the last time, generic combinations of the isolation equations finish each path.</p><p>Each path visits an index once, and\n\\[\n\\sum_i a_i=s^{1/10+o(1)}=o(s^{1/3}),\\qquad\n\\sum_{r&gt;i}a_r&lt;a_i/9.\n\\]\nA terminal piece inside a surviving residual has mass at least\n\\(m\\exp(-a_i/2-\\sum_{r&gt;i}a_r)\\). Any proper test available when extraction stopped captured less than \\(me^{-a_i}\\), so its relative capture is at most\n\\[\n\\exp(-a_i/2+\\sum_{r&gt;i}a_r)\n\\le e^{-7a_i/18}\\to0.\n\\]\nA slightly later grid time absorbs fixed degree factors.</p><p>At width \\(s\\), discarded mass is at most\n\\[\nC_dN(1+\\sqrt{\\log s})e^{-s^{1/10}/2},\n\\]\nplus \\(Ne^{-s^{1/3}}\\). These fractions are summable over dyadic widths, even for arbitrarily slow \\(s_*\\), by reversing widths to \\(2^mu\\) and using \\(2^{m/10}\\ge1+cm\\). Thus total discarded mass is \\(o(N)\\), and every terminal piece has the required size.</p><p>For a first-kind batch, some limiting cut is at most \\(-1/2\\), or its defining polynomial contradicts initial no-capture. For a remainder piece, the last limiting cut is at least \\(1/2\\), or the path contradicts termination of the isolated-batch removal. All estimates are uniform along arbitrary sequences of pieces.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Proposition \\(5.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every non-core limiting profile, \\( \\sum_{i=1}^db_i=0,\\qquad b_1<0<b_d,\\qquad b_1+b_2<0. \\) Put \\( e_j=\\prod_{i\\le j}D_i,\\quad e_0=1,\\qquad \\sigma(b)=\\prod_{i=1}^d\\min(T_s(b),D_i). \\) On a plateau after \\(j\\) cuts …", "statement": "<p>For every non-core limiting profile,\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^db_i=0,\\qquad b_1&lt;0&lt;b_d,\\qquad b_1+b_2&lt;0.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\ne_j=\\prod_{i\\le j}D_i,\\quad e_0=1,\\qquad\n\\sigma(b)=\\prod_{i=1}^d\\min(T_s(b),D_i).\n\\]\nOn a plateau after \\(j\\) cuts, the carrier family has degree at most \\(e_j\\exp(o(s))\\). Any subcollection of degree \\(a\\) touches at most\n\\[\na\\left(\\prod_{i&gt;j}D_i\\right)\\exp(o(s))\n\\]\npiece points. Every fixed positive-density subset \\(S\\) of a piece satisfies\n\\[\nH_S(T_s(b))\\ge\\sigma(b)\\exp(-o(s)),\n\\]\nwith one-sided limiting interpretation at cuts.</p>", "proof": "<p>Successive proper Bézout intersections give the degree and mass budgets, and\n\\[\nn\\le\\left(\\prod_iD_i\\right)\\exp(o(s)),\n\\]\nso \\(\\sum b_i\\ge0\\).</p><p>Propagate rank through plateaux. Before the first cut no testing-degree polynomial vanishes on a dense subset, so its envelope is ambient. On a plateau of dimension \\(l\\), no-capture forces at least one local carrier through almost every selected point into the testing envelope. Assign each such carrier to a containing envelope component and group by dimension. A group of dimension \\(h\\ge l\\) and degree \\(a\\) carries a fixed positive fraction \\(S'\\). Proposition \\(1\\) gives\n\\[\nH_S(T)\\gtrsim aT^h,\\qquad H_{S'}(T')\\lesssim a(T'+1)^h,\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\nH_S(T)\\gtrsim H_{S'}(T')\\bigl(T/(T'+1)\\bigr)^h.\n\\]\nBorrow the previous bound just before the plateau and approach the boundary arbitrarily closely. Since \\(h\\ge l\\), this gives the required growth of \\(\\sigma\\). If the first degree is only \\(\\exp(o(s))\\), start with rank one. After the final cut, rank at most \\(N\\) gives \\(\\prod_iD_i\\le N\\exp(o(s))\\), hence zero sum.</p><p>The profile is nonzero by Lemma \\(9.1\\). Monotonicity and zero sum imply \\(b_1&lt;0&lt;b_d\\). If \\(b_1+b_2\\ge0\\), then \\(b_2&gt;0\\) and the later \\(d-2\\) entries force a positive sum, since \\(d&gt;2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "Proposition \\(5.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Inside an open plateau, separate proper cuts of degree \\(O(T_s(b))\\) on the carriers collectively capture \\(o(n)\\) piece points.", "statement": "<p>Inside an open plateau, separate proper cuts of degree \\(O(T_s(b))\\) on the carriers collectively capture \\(o(n)\\) piece points. At a slightly later interior time there are common equations vanishing on the entire carrier family, with full codimension Jacobian rank at all but \\(o(n)\\) points. Those points have a unique smooth carrier, with tangent space testable by equations and minors at the same interior scale.</p><p>The conclusions hold for two such families, or for unions from \\(\\exp(o(s))\\) pieces of common width and limiting profile, retaining each piece’s own mass accounting. Nonreal flat carriers have negligible assigned mass.</p>", "proof": "<p>A carrier is an irreducible component of its path locus: a larger containing component through a counted point would contradict the local dimension given by proper cuts.</p><p>Let the plateau start at \\(\\beta\\), and choose \\(b&lt;b'&lt;b''\\) inside it. Proposition \\(2\\) pools the separate cuts at degree \\(T_s(b')\\), except on components of degree at most\n\\[\ne_j\\exp(-(b'-b)s+o(s)).\n\\]\nThe mass budget removes all points touching these components at cost \\(o(n)\\), and no-capture at \\(b'\\) removes the remaining zeros.</p><p>Common regularity from Proposition \\(2\\), using the path-system bound \\(B\\exp((\\beta+o(1))s)\\), fails generically on degree at most\n\\[\ne_j\\exp(-(b'-\\beta)s+o(s)).\n\\]\nRemove their mass. A generic combination of maximal minors is proper on every remaining component; apply the individual-cut conclusion with the gap up to \\(b''\\). Full rank gives uniqueness and smoothness.</p><p>For pooled pieces, the effective equation bound is the sum of their bounds, still \\(B\\exp((\\beta+o(1))s)\\). Use each original piece’s later cuts only for its own points. Uniform negligible fractions sum without a factor equal to the number of pieces. Finally, the real points of a nonreal complex flat lie in a proper linear section of that flat; apply the individual-cut conclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition \\(5.5\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition \\(9\\), suppose every \\(p\\in P\\) has a directional family \\(C_p\\) of degree at most \\(E=\\delta N/A\\), each component spanning projective dimension at most two, and all but \\(o(N^2)\\) …", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition \\(9\\), suppose every \\(p\\in P\\) has a directional family \\(C_p\\) of degree at most \\(E=\\delta N/A\\), each component spanning projective dimension at most two, and all but \\(o(N^2)\\) unordered pairs satisfy\n\\[\n\\pi_p(q)\\in C_p\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\pi_q(p)\\in C_q.\n\\]\nAfter one global terminal-curve deletion, either:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. a core of positive relative size is covered;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. two non-core pieces have a common dyadic width, the same limiting profile, and an ordered product covered in one orientation with fixed positive density;</p><p class=\"li num\">3. two non-core pieces have width ratio tending to infinity, both widths \\(o(\\log B)\\), the same limiting profile, and such a covered ordered product.</p><p>At the common outermost width \\(s=\\log B\\), both factors may instead be one pool of \\(\\exp(o(s))\\) pieces in a shrinking profile bin, with size\n\\[\nn\\ge N\\sqrt\\delta,\\qquad n=N\\exp(-o(s)).\n\\]\nAll preceding profile, mass, regularity, and product-testing estimates persist. Selected incidences may be required to lie on physical lines containing at most \\(B^{o(1)}\\) sample points.</p><p>The terminal deletion ensures that whenever a target is assigned to its carrier curve after the \\((d-1)\\)-st cut, that curve does not project into the center’s directional family at a retained incidence.</p>", "proof": "<p>Assign each non-core point to one terminal carrier curve. Omit nonreal lines; each contains at most one real point, and their fraction in a piece is at most\n\\[\ne_{d-1}\\exp(o(s))/n=D_d^{-1}\\exp(o(s))=o(1).\n\\]\nPool repetitions and apply Proposition \\(8\\) once globally, deleting \\(o(N^2)\\) ordered incidences.</p><p>A positive core is already contradictory: its isolation equations, restricted to the at-most-two-dimensional directional cones, give by Bézout at most \\(O(EL^2)=o(N)\\) partners per center. Otherwise discard the core.</p><p>For fixed precision \\(1/h\\), bin limiting profiles and \\(s/\\log B\\) into finitely many bins, and bin dyadic indices modulo an integer tending to infinity with \\(h\\). Two independent retained points agree in one bin with probability bounded below depending on \\(h\\). Conditional uncovered probability tends to zero. Select a pair of pieces, then an orientation carrying at least half the covered pairs. Increase \\(h\\) slowly by a diagonal choice. The selected dyadic indices are equal or their difference tends to infinity. In the latter case equal limiting normalized widths force both widths to be \\(o(\\log B)\\).</p><p>If the outermost window has positive mass, use its largest profile-bin pool in both roles. Increase precision slowly enough that its mass is at least \\(N\\sqrt\\delta\\) and \\(N\\exp(-o(\\log B))\\). There are only \\(\\exp(o(s))\\) constituent pieces.</p><p>Finally Lemma \\(4.2\\) bounds the fraction of pairs on lines of occupancy above \\(H\\) by\n\\[\nO(N^2/H+Nh_{\\max})/N^2=o(1)+O(1/H).\n\\]\nChoose \\(H\\) after each fixed conditioning precision, then diagonalize sufficiently slowly to have \\(H\\le B^{o(1)}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.3\\), and the comparison following equations \\((6.6)\\)–\\((6.7)\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Take the common-window pieces or pool from Proposition \\(10\\), with profile notation from Lemma \\(9.2\\).", "statement": "<p>Take the common-window pieces or pool from Proposition \\(10\\), with profile notation from Lemma \\(9.2\\). For any dense ordered graph \\(\\Gamma\\) of distinct point pairs, not necessarily covered, define\n\\[\n\\psi(x,y)=(x,\\pi_x(y)),\\qquad H(T)=H_{\\psi(\\Gamma)}(T).\n\\]\nAt fixed interior times before the last cut,\n\\[\nH(T_s(b))\\ge\n\\sigma(b)^2\\min\\left(1,\\frac{T_s(b)}{D_1D_2}\\right)\\exp(-o(s)).\n\\]\nThe assertion is uniform over fixed positive-density subgraphs.</p><p>For a graph covered in one orientation,\n\\[\nH(T)\\le CNE(T+1).\n\\]\nThese bounds contradict coverage unless \\(s=\\log B\\) and \\(b_d=1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Before the first cut, product testing forces the envelope of a dense label graph to be the whole label space, of rank comparable to \\(T^{2d-1}\\). Since \\(D_1D_2\\ge T^2\\), this gives the claimed lower bound.</p><p>On a plateau of physical dimension \\(k\\), use Lemma \\(11.1\\) if \\(k&lt;d-1\\). For \\(k=d-1\\), use common physical regularity and delete generic tangent pairs directly by the flat cap. Product testing puts complete \\(2k\\)-dimensional product images into the graph’s envelope at almost all graph pairs.</p><p>Assign each distinct image to a containing envelope component and group by dimension. A group of dimension \\(h\\ge2k\\), degree \\(a\\), carries a dense subgraph. If \\(h\\ge2k+1\\), the Hilbert estimates\n\\[\nH(T)\\gtrsim aT^h,\\qquad H_{\\psi(\\Gamma')}(T')\\lesssim a(T'+1)^h\n\\]\npropagate the previous bound; the required growth exponent is at most \\(2k+1\\).</p><p>If \\(h=2k\\), the images equal the chosen components. For \\(k=d-1\\), the images are \\(X\\times\\mathbb P^{d-1}\\), so a positive source mass gives\n\\[\na\\gtrsim D_1e^{-o(s)}.\n\\]\nThis suffices since \\(T\\le D_2\\) and\n\\[\nD_1^2T^{2d-2}\\min(1,T/(D_1D_2))\n\\le D_1T^{2d-2}.\n\\]\nFor \\(k&lt;d-1\\), Lemma \\(11.1\\) gives \\(a\\gtrsim e_j^2e^{-\\varepsilon s-o(s)}\\). Arbitrary fixed \\(\\varepsilon\\) gives the asserted exponential accuracy. Rank one supplies an initial degree-\\(\\exp(o(s))\\) boundary if needed.</p><p>For coverage, specialize polynomial restrictions at each actual center and use the curve Hilbert upper bound, giving \\(H(T)\\le CNE(T+1)\\). Near the last cut, \\(\\sigma\\) is \\(N\\) within arbitrary logarithmic tolerance. The lower-to-upper ratio is at least, to that accuracy,\n\\[\n\\min\\left(\\frac A{\\delta D_d},\\frac A{\\delta D_1D_2}\\right).\n\\]\nThe second term has a strict \\(s\\)-scale margin by \\(b_1+b_2&lt;0\\). The first does unless \\(s=\\log B,b_d=1\\): any other dyadic width is at most \\(\\frac12\\log B\\), and in the outer width its margin is \\(1-b_d\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix a common plateau after \\(j\\) cuts, with dimension \\(k=d-j<d-1\\), and an interior time \\(\\tau\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix a common plateau after \\(j\\) cuts, with dimension \\(k=d-j&lt;d-1\\), and an interior time \\(\\tau\\). For every fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), delete negligible point sets and carrier pairs. Each retained point has a unique smooth carrier in the full union of the two physical families; flats are real, repeated carriers are pooled, and common physical equations and one maximal-minor combination \\(g_{\\rm ph}\\), of degrees \\(O(T_s(\\tau))\\), have full rank and are nonzero at retained points.</p><p>For retained carrier pairs \\(\\mathcal R\\), put\n\\[\nZ_{X,Y}=\\overline{\\psi(X\\times Y)},\\qquad\nU=\\bigcup_{\\text{distinct }Z_{X,Y}}Z_{X,Y}.\n\\]\nEvery image has dimension \\(2k\\), with both generic endpoint tangent projections injective, and\n\\[\n\\deg U\\le e_j^2\\exp(o(s)).\n\\]\nFor every image subfamily \\(\\mathcal J\\), including one chosen later,\n\\[\n\\frac1{m_xm_y}\n\\sum_{\\substack{(X,Y)\\in\\mathcal R\\\\Z_{X,Y}\\in\\mathcal J}}n_Xn_Y\n\\le\n\\frac{\\deg(\\bigcup_{Z\\in\\mathcal J}Z)}{e_j^2}\n\\exp(\\varepsilon s+o(s)).\n\\]\nFor a common pool, the same deletions and assignment work in both roles.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Lemma \\(9.3\\) at several fixed interior times below \\(\\tau\\), retaining supported assignments before later product tests. Pool identical carriers. The budgets give\n\\[\n\\sum_X\\deg X,\\sum_Y\\deg Y\\le e_j\\exp(o(s)),\n\\]\n\\[\nn_X/m_x\\le(\\deg X/e_j)\\exp(o(s)),\\qquad\nn_Y/m_y\\le(\\deg Y/e_j)\\exp(o(s)).\n\\]\nApply Proposition \\(7\\), with \\(w=s\\), \\(e=e_j\\). Its fixed target lists remove negligible product weight and have generic depth at most \\(\\exp(\\varepsilon s)\\). Target transversality gives image dimension \\(2k\\); isolated-intersection Bézout for the bounded-degree map gives\n\\[\n\\deg Z_{X,Y}\\le C_d\\deg X\\deg Y.\n\\]</p><p>Images with different source carriers are distinct. Fix \\(X\\) and an image \\(Z\\). After projective closure in the direction coordinate, its geometric generic source fiber is the integral projected image of \\(Y_{\\mathbb C(X)}\\). Generic flatness and geometric integrality after shrinking give complete fibers of constant degree \\(d_Z\\). All targets with this image have that same projection at a common general \\(x\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_{Y:Z_{X,Y}=Z}t_x(Y)\\le e^{\\varepsilon s}.\n\\]\nThe point-projection formula gives \\(\\deg Y\\le2t_x(Y)d_Z\\); multiplicity is zero unless \\(X=Y\\), and then is one at general \\(x\\), with coincident flat pairs already deleted.</p><p>A mixed section by \\(k\\) general source hyperplanes and \\(k\\) general direction hyperplanes has \\(\\deg X\\,d_Z\\) points. These are isolated intersections of linear equations on the ordinary projective image closure, so their multiplicity sum is bounded by its degree. Summing over any chosen subfamily yields\n\\[\n\\sum_{Z_{X,Y}\\in\\mathcal J}\\deg X\\deg Y\n\\le C_de^{\\varepsilon s}\\deg\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{Z\\in\\mathcal J}Z\\Bigr).\n\\]\nMultiply the normalized weight bounds. The lists remain fixed when \\(\\mathcal J\\) is chosen.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition \\(10\\), the common outermost endpoint \\( s=\\log B,\\qquad b_d=1 \\) also cannot have a positive-density covered graph retaining the terminal deletion and light-line restriction.", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition \\(10\\), the common outermost endpoint\n\\[\ns=\\log B,\\qquad b_d=1\n\\]\nalso cannot have a positive-density covered graph retaining the terminal deletion and light-line restriction.</p>", "proof": "<p>Retain the covered graph after Lemma \\(12.1\\); it still has \\(\\gtrsim n^2\\) pairs. At a center \\(p\\), any component \\(\\gamma\\subset C_p\\) not contained in the specialized label equations contributes at most \\(O(\\deg\\gamma\\,B^{2-c})\\) directions by Bézout. Light supporting lines have occupancy \\(B^{o(1)}\\), so their target fraction is at most\n\\[\nCEB^{2-c+o(1)}/n\\le C\\sqrt\\delta\\,B^{-c+o(1)}=o(1).\n\\]\nDiscard them.</p><p>At a remaining marked pair \\((p,q)\\), with unique carriers \\(X,Y\\), the product map is locally biholomorphic onto the smooth common \\(2k\\)-fold. Its inverse identifies the reduced germ of \\(Y\\cap\\pi_p^{-1}(\\gamma)\\) with the pure one-dimensional germ of \\(\\gamma\\). An irreducible algebraic component through an inverse branch is therefore a physical curve \\(C\\subset Y\\) with \\(\\overline{\\pi_pC}=\\gamma\\).</p><p>For \\(k=1\\), this is the terminal carrier itself. Its identity with the global assignment follows from uniqueness in the full original physical family; globally unassigned nonreal lines were deleted. This contradicts the terminal deletion.</p><p>For \\(k\\ge2\\), choose lifts for all marked targets over \\(\\gamma\\), and pool repeats. All physical, label, and tangent tests are nonzero on a nonempty open part of each lift. Choose one general direction of \\(\\gamma\\), avoiding all exceptional loci and intersections of distinct lifts. The sum of generic map degrees counts distinct roots.</p><p>There is at most one root. All products use the same source carrier \\(X\\), unique at \\(p\\), and lie in the same smooth label system. Two roots would, by the local assertion of Lemma \\(7.1\\), give\n\\[\n\\overline{T_pX}\\subseteq\\overline{T_{q'}Y}.\n\\]\nBoth spaces have dimension \\(k\\), contradicting the tangent-inequality test. Hence\n\\[\n\\sum_C\\deg(C\\to\\gamma)\\le1.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(p\\in C\\subset Y\\), uniqueness at \\(p\\) forces \\(Y=X\\). The source tangent test and Lemma \\(3.5\\) give \\(\\operatorname{mult}_pC\\le\\deg C/2\\). If \\(p\\notin C\\), multiplicity is zero. Thus\n\\[\n\\deg\\gamma\\,\\deg(C\\to\\gamma)\n=\\deg C-\\operatorname{mult}_pC\\ge\\tfrac12\\deg C.\n\\]\nTotal lift degree over \\(\\gamma\\) is at most \\(2\\deg\\gamma\\). Lemma \\(1.1\\) bounds each row by \\(CEM\\), and\n\\[\nEM/n\\le C\\sqrt\\delta\\,M/A\\to0.\n\\]\nThis contradicts the surviving dense graph.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(k\\) be the dimension of the final open plateau, \\(j=d-k\\), and \\(\\beta=b_j<1\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(k\\) be the dimension of the final open plateau, \\(j=d-k\\), and \\(\\beta=b_j&lt;1\\). Then\n\\[\n1\\le k&lt;d-1,\\qquad e_j=B^{d-2k},\\qquad N/e_j=A^k.\n\\]\nChoose fixed times\n\\[\n\\beta&lt;\\tau&lt;t_0&lt;t_1&lt;t_2&lt;1,\\qquad\nt_0&gt;\\max\\{\\beta,1+b_1+b_2\\},\n\\]\nand \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;(t_1-t_0)/4\\). Apply Lemma \\(11.1\\) to the common pool. If \\(k\\ge2\\), also remove carriers spanning dimension at most \\(k+1\\). Let \\(S\\) be the resulting pool, of size\n\\[\nn=(1-o(1))n_0\\ge(1-o(1))N\\sqrt\\delta,\\qquad n=N\\exp(-o(s)),\n\\]\nand fix the retained product family \\(\\mathcal R,U\\).</p><p>There is a subfamily \\(\\mathcal R_{\\rm sm}\\) omitting assigned product mass \\(o(n^2)\\), and common label equations of degree at most \\(B^{2-c}\\), for some fixed \\(c&gt;0\\), containing all its complete product images. Outside \\(o(n^2)\\) further actual pairs, their common locus is smooth of dimension \\(2k\\), the product map has differential rank \\(2k\\), and both tangent projections are injective. If \\(k\\ge2\\), the projected tangent spaces are unequal. All polynomial tests have degree at most \\(B^{2-c}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The low-span carrier deletion costs at most\n\\[\nB^{d-2k}B^{k+1+o(1)}=B^{d-k+1+o(1)}=o(n_0)\n\\]\nwhen \\(k\\ge2\\). Restricting the fixed lists preserves the subfamily weight estimate of Lemma \\(11.1\\).</p><p>Put \\(T_i=T_s(t_i)\\), \\(V=\\operatorname{Env}_{T_0}(U)\\), and assign every image to one containing envelope component. If images assigned to dimensions \\(h\\ge2k+1\\) represented positive product mass, form the auxiliary dense graph of all assigned actual pairs. Proposition \\(11\\), which does not require coverage, and Hilbert propagation from just before \\(\\beta\\), give\n\\[\nH_V(T_0)\\ge e_j^2T_0^{2k}\n\\min\\left\\{\\frac{T_0}{T_s(\\beta)},\\frac{T_0}{D_1D_2}\\right\\}\ne^{-\\xi s-o(s)}.\n\\]\nChoose fixed tolerances smaller than the positive gaps\n\\(t_0-\\beta\\) and \\(t_0-(1+b_1+b_2)\\). This contradicts\n\\[\nH_V(T_0)=H_U(T_0)\\le Ce_j^2T_0^{2k}e^{o(s)}.\n\\]\nThus almost all represented mass lies on actual \\(2k\\)-dimensional components of \\(V\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition \\(2\\) to that image family at degree \\(T_1\\). Exceptional image degree is at most\n\\[\ne_j^2e^{-(t_1-t_0)s+o(s)}.\n\\]\nThe fixed subfamily estimate of Lemma \\(11.1\\) makes its represented mass \\(o(n^2)\\). The common maximal-minor test pulls back with degree \\(O(T_1)\\); product testing below \\(t_2\\) removes its actual zeros.</p><p>Use the common physical equations from before \\(\\tau\\). For \\(v=q-p\\), transversality at either endpoint is tested by the polynomials \\(\\nabla f_a(r)\\cdot v\\). Generic linear combinations are nonzero on every retained product, so product testing removes actual failures.</p><p>For \\(k\\ge2\\), retained carriers span dimension at least \\(k+2\\). Lemma \\(7.1\\) excludes generic equality of projected tangents. With \\(a_i(r)=\\nabla f_i(r)\\), the rows\n\\[\n(a_i(r)\\cdot v)a_j(r)-(a_j(r)\\cdot v)a_i(r)\n\\]\nspan the annihilator of \\(T_r+\\mathbb Cv\\). Their combined rank tests detect inequality of the two projected tangents. Product testing again removes failures. All degrees are \\(O(T_s(t_2))\\le B^{2-c}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition \\(6.5\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Take the separated-window pieces \\(P_x,P_y\\) from Proposition \\(10\\), with source width \\(v\\), target width \\(s\\), both \\(o(\\log B)\\), and \\(v/s\\to0\\) or \\(v/s\\to\\infty\\).", "statement": "<p>Take the separated-window pieces \\(P_x,P_y\\) from Proposition \\(10\\), with source width \\(v\\), target width \\(s\\), both \\(o(\\log B)\\), and \\(v/s\\to0\\) or \\(v/s\\to\\infty\\). Omit globally unassigned targets. Their limiting profile is the same. Use target notation\n\\[\nD_i=Be^{b_is},\\quad e_j=\\prod_{i\\le j}D_i,\\quad l=d-j.\n\\]\nOn each positive-dimensional target plateau after at least one cut, fix containing \\(l\\)-dimensional carrier assignments, using the persistent global assignment on the final curve plateau. Pool repeated carriers.</p><p>For each fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), delete center–target-component incidences of normalized weight \\(o(1)\\), where \\((p,Y)\\) has weight \\(n_Y/(|P_x||P_y|)\\), and then \\(o(|P_x|)\\) centers. Every retained component projects with dimension \\(l\\). Every retained target subcollection carrying any fixed positive fraction of \\(|P_y|\\) has distinct projected degree at least\n\\[\n(e_j/R)e^{-\\varepsilon s}.\n\\]\nValid denominators are\n\\[\nR=D_1\n\\]\nin either orientation;\n\\[\nR=B\\exp\\left(-s\\sum_{i&gt;j}b_i\\right)\n\\]\nwhen \\(v/s\\to0\\); and\n\\[\nR=Be^{-2s}\n\\]\nwhen \\(v/s\\to\\infty\\), \\(b_{j+1}&gt;0\\), and \\(l&lt;d-1\\).</p><p>For any finite batch of plateau and tolerance choices, deletions are made independently of later fixed positive density thresholds and of later adaptive subcollections.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Lemma \\(13.1\\) for the first denominator and Lemma \\(13.2\\) for narrower centers. For wider centers, \\(q=o(r)\\) because \\(s=o(\\log B)\\). For each fixed target \\(Y\\), Lemma \\(13.3\\) supplies proper cuts separately on the source carriers. Lemma \\(9.3\\) makes the total captured source mass \\(o(|P_x|)\\), uniformly in \\(Y\\). Weighted averaging removes \\(o(1)\\) incidence mass and leaves depth at most \\(r\\).</p><p>If retained groups have depth at most \\(Re^{\\eta s}\\), a subcollection of physical degree \\(a\\) has distinct projected degree at least\n\\[\na/(2Re^{\\eta s}),\n\\]\nby the multiplicity estimate in Lemma \\(13.1\\). Combine with the positive-mass degree budget, choosing \\(\\eta\\) below the requested tolerance.</p><p>Whenever deleted incidence mass is \\(\\alpha=o(1)\\), discard at most \\(\\sqrt\\alpha\\) of centers so each remaining row loses at most \\(\\sqrt\\alpha\\) target mass. This single row deletion serves every later fixed positive-density subcollection, including an adaptively chosen exceptional family; no union bound over subcollections is taken.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition \\(13\\), after negligible incidence deletion, the combined generic projection depth of target components sharing one projected image is at most \\( D_1e^{o(s)}. \\)", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition \\(13\\), after negligible incidence deletion, the combined generic projection depth of target components sharing one projected image is at most\n\\[\nD_1e^{o(s)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The target budgets give\n\\[\n\\sum_Y\\deg Y\\le e_je^{o(s)},\\qquad\n\\sum_{Y\\in\\mathcal A}n_Y\n\\le\\deg(\\mathcal A)\\prod_{i&gt;j}D_i\\,e^{o(s)}.\n\\]\nThus positive assigned mass requires degree at least \\(e_je^{-o(s)}\\).</p><p>Dimension-dropping centers lie in a fixed general tangent flat of each target and are negligible by the flat cap. By Lemma \\(3.4\\), centers with \\(\\operatorname{mult}_pY&gt;\\deg Y/2\\) lie in a bounded-degree hypersurface; initial source no-capture makes them negligible. At retained incidences,\n\\[\nt_p(Y)\\deg\\overline{\\pi_pY}\\ge\\tfrac12\\deg Y.\n\\]</p><p>Use the first target plateau after a drop, whose equations have degree at most \\(D_1e^{o(s)}\\). Its relevant carrier is unique and smooth at almost all targets. If a generic joining ray were contained in this first system, its direction at every assigned regular target would be tangent there. The flat cap and averaging delete these incidences. Every other generic ray meets the system in at most \\(D_1e^{o(s)}\\) distinct roots, bounding combined depth.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the narrower-center case \\(v/s\\to0\\), after negligible incidence deletion the combined depth is at most \\( B\\exp\\left(-s\\sum_{i>j}b_i\\right)e^{\\eta s} \\) for every fixed \\(\\eta>0\\), eventually.", "statement": "<p>In the narrower-center case \\(v/s\\to0\\), after negligible incidence deletion the combined depth is at most\n\\[\nB\\exp\\left(-s\\sum_{i&gt;j}b_i\\right)e^{\\eta s}\n\\]\nfor every fixed \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), eventually.</p>", "proof": "<p>The source points are isolated components of equations of degree\n\\(L=B\\exp(o(s))\\), and \\(|P_x|=N\\exp(o(s))\\), with signed error.</p><p>Fix a target \\(Y\\), and choose \\(y\\) general on it simultaneously for all actual centers. For each center, the additional roots on components with the same projected image are generic, avoid other target components, and number the combined depth minus one.</p><p>Parameterize\n\\[\np=y+t(z-y),\\qquad z\\in\\bigcup_{Z\\in\\mathcal Y}Z.\n\\]\nAt counted solutions, \\(p\\) is isolated in the source system and the ray has finite target fiber, so the pulled-back solution is isolated. The \\((z,t)\\)-variety has dimension \\(l+1\\), degree \\(O_d(e_je^{o(s)})\\), and pulled-back equation degrees at most \\(2L\\). Isolated Bézout bounds the number over all centers by\n\\[\nCe_je^{o(s)}L^{l+1}.\n\\]\nAfter division by \\(|P_x|\\), this is\n\\[\n(e_jB^{l+1}/N)e^{o(s)}\n=B\\exp\\left(-s\\sum_{i&gt;j}b_i\\right)e^{o(s)}.\n\\]\nThe fixed root adds one only. Markov with slack \\(e^{\\eta s}\\), followed by weighted averaging in \\(Y\\), proves the claim.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the wider-center case of Proposition \\(13\\), choose a source plateau just to the right of zero and before the first strictly positive source drop.", "statement": "<p>In the wider-center case of Proposition \\(13\\), choose a source plateau just to the right of zero and before the first strictly positive source drop. Its carrier dimension is\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{i:b_i&gt;0\\}\\ge l,\\qquad k&lt;d.\n\\]\nAfter negligible assignment and incidence deletion, every real-flat source carrier \\(X\\) has a fixed target list \\(\\mathcal Y_X\\) whose generic combined depth is at most \\(q=B^{1/2}\\). Every excessive actual center, meaning retained depth greater than\n\\[\nr=\\lceil Be^{-2s}\\rceil,\n\\]\nlies in a proper cut on its assigned \\(X\\), of degree \\(B\\exp(O(s))\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>All proper component cuts of degree \\(B\\exp(Cs)\\), fixed \\(C\\), are negligible on the source plateau, since \\(s=o(v)\\); leave two fixed positive source-time gaps for testing and pooling.</p><p>Fix supported source assignments, delete nonreal flat assignments in both factors, and pool repeats. Their sizes are \\(N\\exp(-o(\\log B))\\). If \\(k=l\\), the number and weights of flat members are \\(B^{d-l+o(1)}\\) and \\(B^{-(d-l)+o(1)}\\). Proposition \\(7\\), with scale \\(\\log B\\), gives fixed lists of generic depth at most \\(q\\).</p><p>Write the full target path locus as \\(Z(f_1,\\ldots,f_m)\\), with degrees \\(L_0=B\\exp(O(s))\\), retaining all omitted components in the equations. For fixed \\(X,Y\\in\\mathcal Y_X\\), choose \\(y\\in Y\\) general so the generic list bound holds for generic \\(x\\in X\\), and so fibers over \\(\\pi_py\\), for every relevant actual center and target, are affine, distinct, different from endpoints, and on no other component of the full locus.</p><p>Choose \\(L\\ge\\max(L_0,B)\\), \\(L=B\\exp(O(s))\\). Homogenize each target equation to its own degree and take all monomial multiples to degree \\(L\\), keeping the projective base locus, including infinity. On\n\\[\n\\iota_x([U:V])=[U:Uy+V(x-y)],\n\\]\nthese become binary forms of degree \\(L\\), with ambient coefficient degrees at most \\(L\\). The generic line is not contained in the base locus by nontangency at \\(y\\).</p><p>Over \\(\\mathbb C(X)\\), let their gcd have degree \\(g\\). Two generic constant linear combinations \\(F_x,G_x\\) have exactly this gcd. Their \\(2L\\)-square Sylvester matrix \\(S(x)\\) has generic rank\n\\[\n\\rho=2L-g.\n\\]\nAt an excessive center \\(p\\), at least \\(r-O(1)\\) distinguished common roots lie only on their retained target components.</p><p>Specialize the generic gcd along an irreducible curve through \\(p\\) meeting the good open set, normalize the curve, and use its DVR. A primitive integral gcd specializes to a nonzero degree-\\(g\\) form dividing both specialized forms, by the content law. Its limiting divisor has length \\(g\\). At most \\(q\\) distinguished roots lie in its support: after splitting roots over a finite extension, any branch specializing to a distinguished point must already belong to that point’s unique retained component. Immobile endpoints and infinity cannot specialize there; the generic list bound permits at most \\(q\\) remaining distinct roots.</p><p>Thus the specialized common divisor has degree at least \\(g+r-O(1)-q\\), and\n\\[\n\\rho-\\operatorname{rank}S(p)\\ge r/2.\n\\]\nIf both forms vanish, the same follows from \\(\\rho\\ge L\\ge r\\). Binary forms retain roots at infinity, so leading-coefficient loss creates no exception.</p><p>A \\(\\rho\\)-minor \\(Q(x)\\) nonzero on \\(X\\) has degree at most \\(2L^2\\) and ambient order at least \\(r/2\\) at every excessive center, by Lemma \\(3.2\\). Choose a test point of \\(X\\) where it is nonzero. Lemma \\(3.1\\) compresses to a proper cut of degree\n\\[\nO(L^2/r)=B\\exp(O(s)).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In the separated-window setting of Propositions \\(10\\) and \\(13\\), no positive-density ordered product can be covered in one orientation while retaining the global terminal deletion.", "statement": "<p>In the separated-window setting of Propositions \\(10\\) and \\(13\\), no positive-density ordered product can be covered in one orientation while retaining the global terminal deletion.</p>", "proof": "<p>For covered directions,\n\\[\nH(T)\\le CE(T+1).\n\\]\nIf Lemma \\(14.1\\) reaches the last endpoint, choose a fixed time just below it with \\(\\sigma/N\\ge e^{-cs/2}\\) to exponential accuracy. The lower-to-upper ratio tends to infinity, at least \\(\\delta^{-1}e^{cs/3}\\).</p><p>The remaining case is \\(v/s\\to0\\) with a final curve plateau \\(b_{d-1}&lt;b_d\\). Choose\n\\[\nb_0&lt;b_{\\rm eq}&lt;b_{\\rm test}&lt;b_d\n\\]\ninside it, with \\(b_0\\) sufficiently close to \\(b_d\\), and put\n\\[\nT_0=T_s(b_0),\\quad T_1=T_s(b_{\\rm eq}),\\quad\n\\eta=b_{\\rm eq}-b_0,\\quad0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;\\eta.\n\\]\nChoose all depth deletions before subsequent envelopes.</p><p>At a typical center set \\(V_p=\\operatorname{Env}_{T_0}(C_p)\\), whose rank is at most \\(CE(T_0+1)\\). A component group of dimension at least two cannot contain a fixed positive target fraction: borrow the preceding strict rank margin and propagate with exponent at least two. Hence labels on all larger components, including their intersections with curves, cost \\(o(|P_y|)\\) per row.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal F_p\\) be every one-dimensional component of \\(V_p\\). Its total degree is \\(O(E)\\), by the envelope lower bound. Proper-cut testing removes targets whose persistent assigned terminal projection is not contained in \\(V_p\\). Every remaining projected carrier has dimension one, and cannot lie in a larger envelope component, so it is a member of \\(\\mathcal F_p\\).</p><p>Proposition \\(2\\), applied to this entire curve family with equation bound \\(T_0\\) and degree \\(T_1\\), has exceptional degree\n\\[\nO(ET_0/T_1)=O(\\delta(N/A)e^{-\\eta s}).\n\\]\nOn this plateau the narrower-center denominator satisfies\n\\[\ne_{d-1}/R=N/A.\n\\]\nEvery physical assigned subcollection of fixed positive mass therefore has projected degree at least \\((N/A)e^{-\\varepsilon s}\\). The exceptional degree is too small, uniformly for these adaptively selected families.</p><p>The common maximal-minor polynomial of degree \\(O(T_1)\\) pulls back properly on retained physical curves. Individual-cut testing below \\(b_{\\rm test}\\) removes its zeros. At a surviving covered label, the covering component \\(\\gamma\\subset C_p\\) also lies in the one-dimensional envelope family. Full Jacobian rank forces it to equal the projected assigned terminal curve through that label. Thus that physical terminal curve projects into \\(C_p\\), contradicting the global terminal deletion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.7\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Make the fixed finite batch of incidence and center deletions in Proposition \\(13\\).", "statement": "<p>Make the fixed finite batch of incidence and center deletions in Proposition \\(13\\). At typical remaining centers, for directions to every dense remaining target subset,\n\\[\nH(T_s(b))\\ge\\frac{\\sigma(b)T_s(b)}A\\,e^{cs}\n\\]\nfor a fixed \\(c&gt;0\\), at fixed interior testing times and one-sided endpoints, through all target plateaux except possibly the final one-dimensional plateau when \\(v/s\\to0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Initially, target no-capture applied to the pullback of a direction polynomial gives rank comparable to \\(T^{d-1}\\). Relative to \\(\\sigma T/A\\), its ratio is\n\\[\nA/T^2=e^{-2bs},\n\\]\nwith a strict margin near \\(b_1&lt;0\\).</p><p>On a plateau of dimension \\(l\\), the individual-cut conclusion puts the complete projection of almost every assigned target carrier into the testing envelope. Assign projected carriers to containing envelope components and choose a dimension group \\(h\\ge l\\) carrying fixed positive target mass. If \\(h&gt;l\\), Hilbert propagation from just before the plateau preserves the required exponent \\(l+1\\).</p><p>If \\(h=l\\), projected carriers equal the chosen components. Proposition \\(13\\) and the component Hilbert bound give\n\\[\nH(T)\\gtrsim T^le_j/R\\,e^{-o(s)}.\n\\]\nThe ratio to \\(e_jT^{l+1}/A\\) is \\(A/(RT)\\). At the next endpoint the normalized logarithms of \\(RD_{j+1}/A\\), for the three denominators, are\n\\[\nb_1+b_{j+1},\\qquad\n-\\sum_{i&gt;j+1}b_i,\\qquad\nb_{j+1}-2.\n\\]\nThe first is strictly negative on the hypersurface plateau and whenever \\(b_{j+1}\\le0\\). For wider centers the third handles all remaining cases with margin at least one. For narrower centers the second has strict margin when \\(l\\ge2\\) and \\(b_{j+1}&gt;0\\). Only \\(l=1\\) leaves zero margin.</p><p>Choose endpoint approximations and fixed tolerances below the finitely many positive margins, decreasing \\(c\\) finitely many times. All statements quantify over every dense restriction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Theorem \\(6.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Under the sequence and lower-dimensional hypotheses of Proposition \\(6\\), there do not exist directional families \\(C_p\\), one for every \\(p\\in P\\), each a union of irreducible projective curves spanning projective …", "statement": "<p>Under the sequence and lower-dimensional hypotheses of Proposition \\(6\\), there do not exist directional families \\(C_p\\), one for every \\(p\\in P\\), each a union of irreducible projective curves spanning projective dimension at most two, with\n\\[\n\\deg C_p\\le\\delta N/A,\\qquad\\delta\\to0,\n\\]\nsuch that all but \\(o(N^2)\\) unordered pairs satisfy\n\\[\n\\pi_p(q)\\in C_p\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\pi_q(p)\\in C_q.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition \\(10\\). Its core alternative is contradictory by the core isolation degree. Proposition \\(11\\) excludes common windows except the outermost endpoint, which Proposition \\(12\\) excludes. Proposition \\(14\\) excludes separated windows, including the final narrower-center curve endpoint. These alternatives exhaust the selection.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Theorem \\(A.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(m\\), there is \\(C_m\\) such that every irreducible \\(V\\subset\\mathbb A^m_{\\mathbb C}\\) of codimension \\(c>0\\) is an irreducible component of \\( Z(f_1,\\ldots,f_c), \\) where \\(f_i\\in I(V)\\) are …", "statement": "<p>For every fixed \\(m\\), there is \\(C_m\\) such that every irreducible \\(V\\subset\\mathbb A^m_{\\mathbb C}\\) of codimension \\(c&gt;0\\) is an irreducible component of\n\\[\nZ(f_1,\\ldots,f_c),\n\\]\nwhere \\(f_i\\in I(V)\\) are nonconstant and\n\\[\n\\prod_{i=1}^c\\deg f_i\\le C_m\\deg V.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For compatible cuts, prove by induction that every component \\(U\\) of the first \\(s\\) cuts containing \\(V\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\deg U\\ge\\gamma_m^s\\prod_{i\\le s}\\delta_i\n\\]\nfor fixed small \\(\\gamma_m&gt;0\\). The case \\(s=0\\), or \\(\\delta_s=1\\), is immediate. Otherwise put\n\\[\nt=\\delta_s-1,\\qquad j=\\max\\{i&lt;s:\\delta_i&lt;\\delta_s\\}.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\delta_j\\le t&lt;\\delta_{j+1}=\\cdots=\\delta_s\\le2t\\). Lemma \\(16.1\\) gives a \\(j\\)-prefix component \\(W\\) with \\(H_W(t)=H_V(t)\\). Proposition \\(1\\), the inductive degree bound, and \\(V\\subset U\\) give\n\\[\nb_m\\deg U\\,t^{m-s}\\ge H_U(t)\\ge H_V(t)\n\\ge a_m\\gamma_m^j\\left(\\prod_{i\\le j}\\delta_i\\right)t^{m-j}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\gamma_m\\le a_m/(2^mb_m)\\). This proves the induction. At \\(s=c\\), \\(V\\) itself is a prefix component, so\n\\[\n\\prod_i\\deg f_i\\le\\prod_i\\delta_i\\le\\gamma_m^{-c}\\deg V.\n\\]\nNo assertion is made that these equations generate the radical ideal or already have independent differentials.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(A.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(1\\le i\\le c\\), let \\(\\delta_i\\) be the least \\(t\\) such that every component of \\(Z(I(V)_{\\le t})\\) containing all of \\(V\\) has codimension at least \\(i\\); put \\(\\delta_0=0\\).", "statement": "<p>For \\(1\\le i\\le c\\), let \\(\\delta_i\\) be the least \\(t\\) such that every component of \\(Z(I(V)_{\\le t})\\) containing all of \\(V\\) has codimension at least \\(i\\); put \\(\\delta_0=0\\). Then\n\\[\n1\\le\\delta_1\\le\\cdots\\le\\delta_c,\n\\]\nand there are \\(f_i\\in I(V)_{\\le\\delta_i}\\) such that every component of \\(Z(f_1,\\ldots,f_i)\\) containing \\(V\\) has codimension exactly \\(i\\).</p><p>For any such cuts, if \\(\\delta_j\\le t&lt;\\delta_{j+1}\\), some component \\(W\\) of the \\(j\\)-cut prefix containing \\(V\\) satisfies\n\\[\nI(W)_{\\le t}=I(V)_{\\le t},\\qquad H_W(t)=H_V(t).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At stage \\(i\\), none of the preceding codimension-\\((i-1)\\) components containing \\(V\\) lies in \\(Z(I(V)_{\\le\\delta_i})\\). A generic polynomial in that restriction space cuts all properly; the principal ideal theorem gives codimension \\(i\\).</p><p>For the second assertion, if no prefix component had \\(I(V)_{\\le t}\\subset I(W)\\), a generic element of \\(I(V)_{\\le t}\\) would cut every one properly. Since all prefix degrees are at most \\(t\\), the degree-\\(t\\) envelope would have codimension at least \\(j+1\\) at all components containing \\(V\\), contradicting \\(t&lt;\\delta_{j+1}\\). The reverse ideal inclusion follows from \\(V\\subset W\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "Proposition \\(A.7\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(E\\) be a complex vector space of dimension \\(e\\ge3\\), and set \\( S=\\bigwedge^2E^*,\\quad n=\\binom e2,\\quad r=e-1,\\quad s_0=\\binom{e-1}{2}. \\) Regard \\(z\\in S\\) as a skew map \\(E\\to E^*\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(E\\) be a complex vector space of dimension \\(e\\ge3\\), and set\n\\[\nS=\\bigwedge^2E^*,\\quad n=\\binom e2,\\quad r=e-1,\\quad s_0=\\binom{e-1}{2}.\n\\]\nRegard \\(z\\in S\\) as a skew map \\(E\\to E^*\\). For \\(v\\ne0\\), put\n\\[\nK_v=\\{z:zv=0\\},\\qquad\nF_{[v:y]}=\\{z:zv=y\\},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nQ=\\{[v:y]\\in\\mathbb P(E\\oplus E^*):y(v)=0\\}.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(V\\subsetneq S\\) be irreducible of dimension \\(s_0+h\\), \\(1\\le h&lt;r\\), and let \\(\\Gamma\\) be a controlled ruling incidence from Lemma \\(17.2\\), with fiber dimension \\(j\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) over its working open subset of \\(V\\). If \\(\\Lambda\\) is the projective closure of its parameter image \\((z,v)\\mapsto[v:zv]\\), then\n\\[\n\\dim\\Lambda=h+j,\\qquad\\deg\\Lambda\\le C_e\\deg V.\n\\]\nIf \\(j=1\\), then \\(h=r-1\\) and \\(\\Lambda\\) is a linear maximal-isotropic \\(\\mathbb P^r\\subset Q\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>On \\(\\mathbb P(E)\\), let \\(L=\\mathcal O(-1)\\), \\(Q_0=(E\\otimes\\mathcal O)/L\\). Evaluation gives\n\\[\n0\\to K\\to S\\otimes\\mathcal O\\to Q_0^*\\otimes L^*\\to0,\n\\qquad K=\\bigwedge^2Q_0^*.\n\\]\nUse projective bundles of lines:\n\\[\nB=\\mathbb P(\\mathcal O\\oplus Q_0^*\\otimes L^*)\n\\simeq\\mathbb P(L\\oplus Q_0^*).\n\\]\nThe morphism\n\\[\n\\mu(L,[t:\\psi])=[tv:\\psi(v)]\n\\]\nis an isomorphism over \\(v\\ne0\\). If \\(T=\\mathcal O_B(-1)\\) in the first presentation, put\n\\[\na=\\rho^*c_1(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P(E)}(1)),\\quad\nb=c_1(T^*),\\quad\\ell=\\mu^*c_1(\\mathcal O_Q(1)).\n\\]\nThen \\(\\ell=a+b\\), and \\(a,\\ell\\) are globally generated.</p><p>Let \\(U\\) be the inverse image of \\(T\\) under evaluation from \\((\\mathbb C\\oplus S)\\otimes\\mathcal O_B\\). Then\n\\[\n0\\to\\rho^*K\\to U\\to T\\to0.\n\\]\nIts projective bundle maps to \\(\\mathbb P(\\mathbb C\\oplus S)\\). Over an affine parameter, its fiber is the projective evaluation flat. Boundary \\(t=0\\) forces point coordinate \\(x_0=0\\), so no boundary parameter occurs over affine points.</p><p>Lift \\(\\Lambda\\) birationally to \\(\\widetilde\\Lambda\\subset B\\), and put \\(X=\\mathbb P(U|_{\\widetilde\\Lambda})\\). The incidence is dense in \\(X\\), and its point map is a surjection \\(f:X\\to\\overline V\\). Let \\(\\xi=f^*c_1(\\mathcal O_{\\overline V}(1))\\). The generic point fiber has \\(\\ell^j\\)-degree \\(d_\\Gamma\\le C_e\\), equal to one for \\(j=1\\): its top-dimensional part is the closure of the smooth finite or affine-line incidence fiber. Hence\n\\[\n\\int_X\\ell^j\\xi^{s_0+h}=d_\\Gamma\\deg V\\le C_e\\deg V.\n\\]</p><p>The line-projective-bundle formula gives\n\\[\n\\pi_*(\\xi^{s_0+h})=s_h(U),\n\\]\nand the tautological sequence gives\n\\[\ns_t(U)=\\frac{(1+at)^e}{(1+2at)(1+(a-\\ell)t)}.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\ns_h(U)=\\sum_{i=0}^h\\ell^ia^{h-i}c(e-1-i,h-i),\n\\qquad\nc(p,u)=[z^u]\\frac{(1+z)^p}{1+2z}.\n\\]\nFor \\(p\\ge u+1\\), these coefficients are nonnegative, using\n\\[\nc(p,u)=\\binom{p-2}{u}+c(p-2,u-2),\n\\]\nwith the stated base cases. The coefficient of \\(\\ell^h\\) is one. Nonnegativity of mixed intersections of globally generated classes yields\n\\[\n\\deg\\Lambda=\\int_{\\widetilde\\Lambda}\\ell^{h+j}\n\\le\\int_{\\widetilde\\Lambda}\\ell^js_h(U)\n\\le C_e\\deg V.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(j=1\\), Lemma \\(17.2\\) gives codimension one, \\(h=r-1\\), \\(\\dim\\Lambda=r\\). At general \\(\\lambda=[v:y]\\), choose \\(z_0v=y\\), put \\(U_0=E/\\mathbb Cv\\), and identify the tangent quadratic space with\n\\[\nU_0\\oplus U_0^*,\\qquad\n(\\dot v,\\dot y)\\mapsto(\\alpha,\\beta)\n=(\\dot v\\bmod v,\\dot y-z_0\\dot v).\n\\]\nThe quadratic form is \\(\\beta(\\alpha)\\). For the \\(r\\)-plane \\(\\Theta=T_\\lambda\\Lambda\\), differentiating the affine evaluation incidence at \\(z=z_0+D\\), \\(D\\in\\bigwedge^2U_0^*\\), gives modulo vertical directions\n\\[\n\\Theta\\to U_0^*,\\qquad(\\alpha,\\beta)\\mapsto\\beta-D\\alpha.\n\\]\nIts determinant vanishes for every skew \\(D\\), because the point image lies in the hypersurface \\(V\\).</p><p>Put \\(B_*=\\Theta\\cap U_0^*\\), \\(A_*=\\operatorname{pr}_{U_0}\\Theta\\), \\(H_*=B_*^\\perp\\), with \\(\\dim A_*=\\dim H_*=p\\). The quotient condition is\n\\[\n\\det(C-D|_{A_*\\times H_*})=0\n\\quad\\text{for all skew }D.\n\\]\nIf \\(A_*\\ne H_*\\), choose bases on their intersection and complementary parts. A restriction matrix may be arbitrary except for its common skew block; an invertible such matrix exists, using a nondegenerate skew block for even intersection dimension and a \\(2\\times2\\) off-diagonal block for odd dimension. Scaling it contradicts the determinant identity. Hence \\(A_*=H_*\\). Even \\(p\\) is similarly impossible. For odd \\(p\\), if \\(C(x,x)\\ne0\\), take a skew form annihilating \\(x\\) and nondegenerate on a complement; the leading coefficient of its scaled determinant is nonzero. Therefore \\(C\\) is skew, and \\(\\Theta\\) is maximal isotropic.</p><p>Finally use a quadric chart\n\\[\nx\\mapsto[e_0+x-q_0(x)f_0].\n\\]\nChoose coordinates \\(q_0(u,w)=u\\cdot u-w\\cdot w\\) with tangent plane \\(w=u\\). Locally write \\(w=F(u)\\). Null tangency says\n\\[\nF_i\\cdot F_j=\\delta_{ij}.\n\\]\nDifferentiation gives\n\\[\n2F_{ij}\\cdot F_l\n=\\partial_i g_{jl}+\\partial_j g_{il}-\\partial_lg_{ij}=0.\n\\]\nThe \\(F_l\\) form a basis, so \\(F_{ij}=0\\). The local submanifold is affine-linear and null; its quadric lift spans a linear isotropic \\(\\mathbb P^r\\). Its analytic open subset is Zariski dense in irreducible \\(\\Lambda\\), so \\(\\Lambda\\) equals that plane.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(A.5\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(V\\subset\\mathbb A^n_{\\mathbb C}\\) be irreducible of codimension \\(c>0\\), and a component of \\(Z(f_1,\\ldots,f_c)\\), with \\( \\deg f_i\\le D,\\qquad\\prod_i\\deg f_i\\le C_n\\deg V. \\) Then there are …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(V\\subset\\mathbb A^n_{\\mathbb C}\\) be irreducible of codimension \\(c&gt;0\\), and a component of \\(Z(f_1,\\ldots,f_c)\\), with\n\\[\n\\deg f_i\\le D,\\qquad\\prod_i\\deg f_i\\le C_n\\deg V.\n\\]\nThen there are \\(g_1,\\ldots,g_c\\in I(V)\\) of degrees \\(O_n(D)\\), generically independent differentials, and a polynomial \\(H_0\\) of degree \\(O_n(D)\\), nonzero on \\(V\\), such that \\(V\\setminus Z(H_0)\\) is smooth with tangent space \\(\\bigcap_i\\ker dg_i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At \\(P=I(V)\\), the regular local ring \\(R=\\mathbb C[x]_P\\) has dimension \\(c\\), residue field \\(K=\\mathbb C(V)\\), and \\((f_i)\\) is a regular parameter ideal \\(J\\). Generic normalization of \\(V\\), followed by a geometric generic fiber, identifies \\(\\deg V\\) isolated complete-intersection points, each with local length \\(\\operatorname{length}_R(R/J)\\). Isolated Bézout gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}_R(R/J)\\le\n\\frac{\\prod_i\\deg f_i}{\\deg V}\\le C_n.\n\\]\nChoose bounded \\(L\\) with \\(\\mathfrak m^L\\subset J\\).</p><p>Construct independent conormals successively. After \\(g_1,\\ldots,g_u\\), the quotient \\(R_u\\) is regular local. Some \\(f_i\\) has finite \\(\\mathfrak m_u\\)-order \\(b\\le L\\), or Nakayama would force \\(\\mathfrak m_u^L=0\\). Choose a nonzero \\(u\\)-Jacobian minor \\(\\Delta\\). For each remaining column \\(J_t\\), the polynomial vector field with coefficient \\(\\Delta\\) in coordinate \\(t\\) and coefficients \\(-\\operatorname{adj}(J_0)J_t\\) in the chosen coordinates annihilates all previous \\(g_i\\).</p><p>These fields span their common tangent kernel; their residue evaluations span the dual of \\(\\mathfrak m_u/\\mathfrak m_u^2\\). Polarization of the leading degree-\\(b\\) term in the regular associated graded ring shows some composition of \\(b\\) fields detects it. Stop one differentiation earlier. The resulting polynomial lies in \\(P\\) and adds a nonzero independent conormal. Boundedly many differentiations and stages keep degrees \\(O_n(D)\\). A nonzero maximal Jacobian minor supplies \\(H_0\\), and the implicit function theorem identifies the local germ.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(A.6\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(V\\subset S\\) have dimension \\(s_0+h\\), \\(1\\le h<r\\), and approximate equations as in Lemma \\(17.1\\), of maximum degree \\(D\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(V\\subset S\\) have dimension \\(s_0+h\\), \\(1\\le h&lt;r\\), and approximate equations as in Lemma \\(17.1\\), of maximum degree \\(D\\). There is a polynomial \\(H\\), nonzero on \\(V\\), of degree \\(O_e(D)\\), such that on \\(\\Omega=V\\setminus Z(H)\\) there are \\(O_e(1)\\) smooth irreducible incidences \\(\\Gamma\\), closed in a normalized direction chart over \\(\\Omega\\), smoothly dominant onto \\(\\Omega\\).</p><p>Their fibers are finite of bounded size or affine lines of normalized vectors; the latter occurs only in codimension one. Every contained evaluation flat meeting \\(\\Omega\\) is represented. Every nonempty parameter fiber is exactly \\(F_\\lambda\\cap\\Omega\\), of dimension \\(s_0\\), so the parameter image has dimension \\(h+j\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use regular equations \\(g_1,\\ldots,g_c\\). Since \\(K_v^\\perp=v\\wedge E\\), the necessary tangent condition is\n\\[\nv\\wedge dg_i(z)=0\\qquad(1\\le i\\le c).\n\\]\nIts vector solution space has dimension at most two: a nonzero bivector annihilated by wedging with \\(v\\) is \\(v\\wedge a\\), and its annihilating vectors are \\(\\operatorname{span}(v,a)\\). Two independent conormals cannot have the same two-dimensional solution space. Thus a two-dimensional solution space forces \\(c=1\\).</p><p>In each chart \\(v_a=1\\), Gaussian elimination over \\(\\mathbb C(V)\\), after deleting nonzero rank minors, gives no solution, a graph \\(v=b(z)\\), or\n\\[\nv=b(z)+\\tau a_1(z),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\tau\\) is a free coordinate. All rational degrees are \\(O_e(D)\\).</p><p>The exterior products of \\(\\epsilon_i-v_i\\epsilon_a\\), \\(i\\ne a\\), give a quadratic-in-\\(v\\) basis \\(B_\\alpha(v)\\) of \\(K_v\\). Test whether\n\\[\nX_\\alpha=(B_\\alpha(v),0)\n\\]\nis tangent to the graph, holding \\(\\tau\\) fixed. The derivative tests are rational in \\(z\\), and polynomial of bounded degree in \\(\\tau\\). Every contained flat passes them. With no free coordinate, remove any nonzero failed test; if all vanish, retain the graph.</p><p>With a free coordinate and nonzero tests, take their gcd over \\(\\mathbb C(V)\\), then its monic squarefree part \\(G(\\tau)\\). Exclude poles of quotient and Bézout identities and its discriminant. The common-root locus is finite étale of bounded degree over \\(\\Omega\\). A second necessary test is\n\\[\nR_\\alpha=\\sum_m(B_\\alpha(v(z,\\tau)))_m\\,\\partial_{z_m}G=0.\n\\]\nTake \\(P_2=\\gcd(G,R_1,\\ldots,R_{s_0})\\), deleting coefficient poles. Its roots form a union of complete étale sheets. On each sheet the horizontal fields are tangent, so no further tangency tests are needed. All computations use boundedly many field operations in bounded \\(\\tau\\)-degree, hence exclude total degree \\(O_e(D)\\).</p><p>Their ambient flows are\n\\[\n(z,v)\\mapsto(z+tB_\\alpha(v),v).\n\\]\nLocal uniqueness of holomorphic flows keeps them in the incidence, producing an analytic open subset of \\(z+K_v\\). Polynomial identity then puts the whole flat in \\(V\\). Intersect the working opens from all charts. Every contained flat meeting the result passes both necessary tests and is represented.</p><p>For fixed parameter, the fiber is closed in \\(F_\\lambda\\cap\\Omega\\) and contains an analytic open subset generated by these flows. It therefore equals the whole irreducible intersection. The fiber-dimension theorem gives \\(\\dim\\Lambda=h+j\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 18", "orig": "Theorem \\(A.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use \\(E,S,Q,r,s_0,F_\\lambda\\) from Proposition \\(17\\).", "statement": "<p>Use \\(E,S,Q,r,s_0,F_\\lambda\\) from Proposition \\(17\\). Fix \\(C_0\\ge1\\), \\(A\\ge1\\), and a finite set \\(\\mathcal P\\subset Q\\) of distinct parameters, all with \\(v\\ne0\\). Assume:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. every parameter has a positive-degree homogeneous separator of degree at most \\(C_0A\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">2. every linear \\(\\mathbb P^r\\subset Q\\) contains at most \\(C_0A^{r-1}\\) sample parameters.</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\lambda\\in\\mathcal P:F_\\lambda\\subset V\\}\n\\le C(e,C_0)\\deg(V)A^h\n\\]\nfor irreducible proper \\(V\\subset S\\) of dimension \\(s_0+h\\), \\(1\\le h&lt;r\\), in either setting:</p><p>(a) all such \\(V\\), provided \\(|\\mathcal P|\\le C_0A^r\\);</p><p>(b) only \\(V\\) contained in a nonzero polynomial zero set of degree at most \\(C_0A\\), without a total-count hypothesis.</p><p>For \\(h=0\\), at most one flat is contained in an irreducible carrier. In (a), the ambient endpoint is the total-count bound. In (b), a degree-\\(D\\le C_0A\\) polynomial contains at most \\(CDA^{r-1}\\) evaluation flats.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a fixed sample define \\(R_h\\) as the supremum of the count divided by \\(\\deg(V)A^h\\) over allowed carriers of dimension \\(s_0+h\\). These suprema are finite. Have \\(R_0\\le1\\), and in (a) \\(R_r\\le C_0\\).</p><p>Take approximate equations from Proposition \\(16\\), with nondecreasing degree bounds \\(d_1,\\ldots,d_c\\) and\n\\[\n\\prod_i d_i\\le C_n\\deg V.\n\\]\nReplace the first by a smallest positive-degree equation in \\(I(V)\\), preserving the remaining degree bounds: in the regular local ring at \\(I(V)\\), generic combinations of the first \\(i\\) original equations extend each replacement regular prefix. Thus \\(V\\) remains a component.</p><p>Choose constants \\(K_1&gt;\\cdots&gt;K_{r-1}&gt;C_0\\). If all \\(d_i\\le K_hA\\), Lemma \\(17.2\\) gives an exceptional section of degree \\(O_e(K_hA)\\). Outside it, boundedly many incidences have parameter degree \\(O_e(\\deg V)\\). For \\(j=0\\), Lemma \\(18.1\\) counts \\(O(\\deg(V)A^h)\\). For \\(j=1\\), Proposition \\(17\\) identifies a maximal-isotropic plane and the cap counts \\(O(A^h)\\). The exceptional section is bounded by \\(R_{h-1}\\), giving\n\\[\nR_h\\le C(1+K_hR_{h-1})\n\\]\nfor this alternative.</p><p>Otherwise let \\(p&lt;c\\) be the prefix length with \\(d_i\\le K_hA\\). A prefix component \\(W\\supset V\\), of codimension \\(p\\), has degree at most \\(\\prod_{i\\le p}d_i\\). Put \\(i=r-p&gt;h\\). Then\n\\[\n\\deg V\\ge C_n^{-1}\\deg W(K_hA)^{i-h},\n\\]\nso the normalized count is at most \\(CR_i/K_h^{i-h}\\). In (b), the minimal first equation has degree at most \\(C_0A\\), hence belongs to the prefix; \\(W\\) is still allowed and \\(i&lt;r\\).</p><p>Thus\n\\[\nR_h\\le\\max\\left\\{C(1+K_hR_{h-1}),\n\\max_{i&gt;h,\\ i\\text{ available}}\\frac{CR_i}{K_h^{i-h}}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(B_0\\ge\\max(1,C_0)\\), \\(c_1&gt;4C+4\\), \\(\\kappa&gt;4Cc_1\\), and \\(K_h=\\kappa K_{h+1}\\), starting with \\(K_{r-1}\\ge\\max(2,C_0+1)\\). Set \\(B_h=c_1K_hB_{h-1}\\), and \\(B_r=B_0\\) in (a). Each transition in the displayed maximum is less than \\(B_h/2\\) when the other \\(R_i\\) are replaced by \\(B_i\\). If \\(\\max_hR_h/B_h&gt;1\\), a maximizing interior index contradicts the same inequalities after scaling by that maximum. Hence all \\(R_h\\) are uniformly bounded.</p><p>Distinct parameters give distinct flats because the common radical of forms in \\(K_v\\) is exactly \\(\\mathbb Cv\\); the direction recovers \\([v]\\), and evaluation recovers \\(y\\). The endpoint and polynomial consequences now follow as stated.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(A.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the separator assumption of Proposition \\(18\\), any irreducible projective variety \\(Y\\subset\\mathbb P(E\\oplus E^*)\\) of dimension \\(a\\) contains at most \\( C_{e,C_0}\\deg(Y)A^a \\) sample parameters.", "statement": "<p>Under the separator assumption of Proposition \\(18\\), any irreducible projective variety \\(Y\\subset\\mathbb P(E\\oplus E^*)\\) of dimension \\(a\\) contains at most\n\\[\nC_{e,C_0}\\deg(Y)A^a\n\\]\nsample parameters.</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on \\(a\\). Choose one sample parameter in \\(Y\\), and use its separator. Its proper zero section contains every other counted parameter and has degree at most \\(C_0A\\deg Y\\). Apply induction to its components and absorb the selected point.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 19", "orig": "Theorem \\(7.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are absolute \\(C_{\\rm rich},K_{\\rm rich}>0\\) such that the following holds.", "statement": "<p>There are absolute \\(C_{\\rm rich},K_{\\rm rich}&gt;0\\) such that the following holds. Let \\(X,Y\\subset\\mathbb R^3\\) have at most \\(N\\) points, put \\(A=N^{2/3}\\), and suppose every line contains at most \\(2A\\) points of either cloud. For every spatial isometry \\(g(x)=Sx+h\\), \\(S\\in O(3)\\), put\n\\[\nk_g=|\\{x\\in X:g(x)\\in Y\\}|.\n\\]\nThe motions with \\(k_g\\ge C_{\\rm rich}A\\) form a finite set, and\n\\[\n\\sum_{g:k_g\\ge C_{\\rm rich}A}k_g^2\n\\le K_{\\rm rich}N^4/A.\n\\]\nIn particular this holds for \\(X=P\\), \\(Y\\) a congruent copy of \\(P\\), when \\(1+|\\Delta(P)|\\le A\\). Both orientations are included.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose\n\\[\n\\alpha=1/14,\\quad\\beta=(4K_{\\rm part})^{-1},\\quad\\nu=\\eta_1/4,\n\\]\nand \\(C_{\\rm rich}\\) sufficiently large for all local thresholds. Every counted motion has a noncollinear matched triple because \\(C_{\\rm rich}&gt;4\\) and the line cap is \\(2A\\); finitely many triples give finitely many motions.</p><p>Bucket by\n\\[\nk\\le k_g&lt;2k,\\qquad k=tA,\\quad t=2^jC_{\\rm rich}\\le N^{1/3}.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nr=t^{1+\\alpha},\\quad m=8N/r,\\quad b=\\beta k/r=\\beta At^{-\\alpha}.\n\\]\nUse Lemma \\(19.4\\). At most \\(K_{\\rm part}r\\) local region-pair counts are nonzero, so discarding counts below \\(b\\) loses at most \\(k/4\\) matches. Bucket the others by \\(\\ell=2^ib\\). The choices ensure\n\\[\nb/m^{2/3}=(\\beta/4)t^{9/14}\\ge C_{\\rm loc},\\qquad\nq=\\eta_1b\\ge\\sqrt N.\n\\]</p><p>For motions with at least \\(k/4\\) retained non-line-type mass, Lemma \\(19.3\\) gives total local mass \\(O(m^3/\\sqrt b)\\) per region pair. Over \\(O(r^2)\\) pairs,\n\\[\n|\\mathcal G_0|k^2\\le Ckr^2m^3/\\sqrt b\n\\le C(N^4/A)t^{-1/28}.\n\\]</p><p>Every remaining motion has at least \\(k/2\\) line-type local mass. Choose a rich source line in each such pair and retain its matches, obtaining disjoint assigned line weights\n\\[\nW_g=\\sum_Lw_{g,L}\\ge\\nu k,\\qquad w_{g,L}\\le2A.\n\\]\nAll occurring source and target lines are \\(q\\)-rich. Lemma \\(4.2\\) and \\(q\\ge\\sqrt N\\) bound each full line list by \\(L_*\\le CN/b\\). Since \\(W_g\\ge4A\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{L_1\\ne L_2}w_{g,L_1}w_{g,L_2}\\ge\\tfrac12\\nu^2k^2.\n\\]\nFor nonparallel prescriptions, Lemma \\(19.5\\) bounds their summed contribution by \\(O(A^2L_*^4)\\). For parallel source pairs, Lemma \\(19.6\\) gives \\(O(L_*^{3/2})\\) target pairs at the prescribed separation; Lemma \\(19.5\\) bounds each translation-family contribution by \\(O(A^3)\\). Hence\n\\[\n|\\mathcal G_1|k^2\n\\le C\\bigl(A^2(N/b)^4+A^3(N/b)^{7/2}\\bigr).\n\\]\nThese terms are \\(O(N^{8/3}t^{2/7})\\) and \\(O(N^{19/6}t^{1/4})\\). Summing increasing geometric buckets up to \\(N^{1/3}\\) gives \\(O(N^{58/21})\\) and \\(O(N^{13/4})\\), both \\(O(N^{10/3})\\). The decreasing non-line-type series gives \\(O(N^4/A)\\). Since \\(k_g&lt;2k\\), actual squared counts cost only a factor four.</p><p>For \\(P\\), each line has at most \\(2M\\le2A\\) points by the elementary line-distance bound, proving the stated specialization.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are absolute \\(\\delta_0,\\gamma_0>0\\) such that a finite set of \\(n\\) distinct real points, with at most \\(\\delta_0n\\) on any line, spans at least \\(\\gamma_0n^2\\) lines.", "statement": "<p>There are absolute \\(\\delta_0,\\gamma_0&gt;0\\) such that a finite set of \\(n\\) distinct real points, with at most \\(\\delta_0n\\) on any line, spans at least \\(\\gamma_0n^2\\) lines. At least \\(\\gamma_0n\\) points each lie on at least \\(\\gamma_0n\\) spanned lines. This holds in fixed real affine dimension and for finite real projective-plane configurations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Project generically to the plane. Lemma \\(4.2\\) gives \\(O(n^2/u^3+n/u)\\) \\(u\\)-rich lines. Dyadic summation from a large fixed \\(u_0\\) to \\(\\delta_0n\\) bounds ordered pairs on those lines by\n\\[\nC(n^2/u_0+\\delta_0n^2).\n\\]\nChoose \\(u_0\\) large and then \\(\\delta_0\\) small. A positive fraction of pairs lie on lines of bounded occupancy, giving \\(\\Omega(n^2)\\) lines. Their incidence sum is \\(\\Omega(n^2)\\), and each point has at most \\(n-1\\) incident spanned lines, proving the anchor assertion. Choose a generic affine chart for projective data.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a cloud \\(X\\subset\\mathbb R^3\\), \\(|X|\\le m\\), define \\( e_X(\\Pi)=|X\\cap\\Pi|-\\max_{L\\subset\\Pi}|X\\cap L|. \\) There are absolute \\(C_2,K_2\\) such that, for \\(H\\ge C_2m^{2/3}\\) …", "statement": "<p>For a cloud \\(X\\subset\\mathbb R^3\\), \\(|X|\\le m\\), define\n\\[\ne_X(\\Pi)=|X\\cap\\Pi|-\\max_{L\\subset\\Pi}|X\\cap L|.\n\\]\nThere are absolute \\(C_2,K_2\\) such that, for \\(H\\ge C_2m^{2/3}\\),\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\Pi:e_X(\\Pi)\\ge H\\}\\le K_2m^2/H^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>If a richest line contains at least half the plane’s sample, both it and the off-line set have at least \\(H\\) points. There are \\(O(m/H)\\) such rich lines, and at most \\(m/H\\) relevant planes through each.</p><p>For the other planes, bucket occupancies in \\([K,2K)\\), \\(K\\ge H\\), and let \\(f\\) be their number. Each spatial line belongs to at most \\(2m/K\\) such planes. If \\(r_x\\) counts bucket planes through \\(x\\), then\n\\[\nJ=\\sum_xr_x(r_x-1)\\ge K^2f^2/m-2Kf.\n\\]\nFix one plane. Intersection lines with occupancy below \\(C_3\\sqrt K\\) contribute at most \\(C_3\\sqrt Kf\\). Lemma \\(4.2\\) gives \\(O(\\sqrt K)\\) richer intersection lines, whose summed occupancies are \\(O(K)\\), since two lines meet once and\n\\[\n\\sum\\text{occupancies}\\le|X\\cap\\Pi|+\\binom a2.\n\\]\nTheir contribution, with plane multiplicity, is \\(O(m)\\). Thus\n\\[\nJ\\le C_5f(m+\\sqrt Kf).\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(C_2\\), combine and absorb to obtain \\(f=O(m^2/K^2)\\). Sum dyadically.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(0<\\eta_1\\ll\\eta_2\\ll1\\) as in the paper, using \\(\\delta_0,\\gamma_0\\) of Lemma \\(19.1\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(0&lt;\\eta_1\\ll\\eta_2\\ll1\\) as in the paper, using \\(\\delta_0,\\gamma_0\\) of Lemma \\(19.1\\). For a bucket \\(\\ell\\le k_g(X,Y)&lt;2\\ell\\), call a motion line type if some matched source line contains at least \\(\\eta_1\\ell\\) matches. Among the others, call it planar type if a plane contains at least \\(\\eta_2\\ell\\), and general type otherwise.</p><p>There are absolute \\(C_{\\rm loc},K_{\\rm loc}\\) such that, for \\(|X|,|Y|\\le m\\) and \\(\\ell\\ge C_{\\rm loc}m^{2/3}\\), the number of non-line-type motions in the bucket is at most\n\\[\nK_{\\rm loc}m^3/\\ell^{3/2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A non-line-type match set contains a noncollinear triple, so the motion list is finite. Separate orientations and reflect when necessary to use reversing motions. Rotate the second cloud generically so every counted motion has one-dimensional \\(\\ker(I+S)\\), and perpendicular projection along that axis preserves all matched noncollinear triples.</p><p>Use \\((w,c)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\oplus\\mathbb R^3\\), \\(Zx=w\\times x\\), with null form \\(q(w,c)=w\\cdot c\\). For a pair put \\(v=(x+y)/2,b=(x-y)/2\\), and\n\\[\nF_{x,y}=\\{(w,c):b=w\\times v+c\\}.\n\\]\nIts direction is maximal null. In positively oriented coordinates with the reversing axis \\(e_3\\), write the motion graph as\n\\[\nv_3=v_0,\\qquad b_\\perp=\\omega Jv_\\perp+d.\n\\]\nThe motion flat is\n\\[\nG_g=\\{w_3=\\omega,\\ c_1+v_0w_2=d_1,\\ c_2-v_0w_1=d_2\\}.\n\\]\nIt too is maximal null. A match gives the intersection plane\n\\[\nF_{x,g(x)}\\cap G_g:\n\\quad \\tau+\\xi v_1+\\eta v_2=b_3,\n\\quad(\\xi,\\eta,\\tau)=(-w_2,w_1,c_3).\n\\]\nThe affine map \\(x\\mapsto(v_1,v_2,b_3)\\) is invertible. Distinct matches give distinct planes in \\(G_g\\). In a fixed pair flat, the incident plane \\(w\\cdot a=\\omega\\) recovers the axis and Cayley parameter, hence \\(S\\), and the match fixes translation. The whole motion flat also recovers the motion from its translation-axis direction, \\(w\\)-projection, coupling, and affine constants.</p><p>Suppose one orientation contains\n\\[\nT=\\left\\lceil K_3m^3/\\ell^{3/2}\\right\\rceil\n\\]\ngeneral-type motions. Restrict to \\(T\\) and prune their bipartite match graph to minimum degrees \\(\\ell/4\\) on motions and \\(T\\ell/(4m^2)\\) on labels. A nonzero ambient polynomial vanishes on all retained motion flats at degree\n\\[\nD\\le C_6K_3^{1/3}m/\\sqrt\\ell.\n\\]\nChoose \\(K_3\\) so the label degree exceeds \\(D\\). More than \\(D\\) distinct incident planes force the polynomial to vanish on every retained pair flat.</p><p>Raise gradients with the null bilinear form. A polynomial vanishing on a maximal-null flat has raised gradient in its direction. At three transverse match planes in \\(G_g\\), the gradient belongs to all three plane directions and hence is zero. There are enough such points: Lemma \\(19.1\\) gives \\(\\Omega(\\ell)\\) anchors, each with \\(\\Omega(\\ell)\\) distinct directions; the plane cap and a second application give \\(\\Omega(\\ell^2)\\) spanned nonvertical planes through each anchor. Graph duality makes these distinct triple-intersection points in its match plane. They lie on at most \\(2\\ell\\) arrangement lines, each containing at most \\(2\\ell\\) such intersection points, so \\(\\Omega(\\ell)\\) lines each contain \\(\\Omega(\\ell)\\) designated points.</p><p>Increase \\(C_{\\rm loc}\\), after fixing \\(K_3\\), so \\(D\\) is smaller than all these richness constants times \\(\\ell\\). Every derivative vanishes on the rich lines, then on the anchor planes, then on \\(G_g\\), and propagates to pair flats. Repeat degree descent to a nonzero constant derivative vanishing on a nonempty flat, a contradiction.</p><p>For planar type, choose supporting planes \\(\\Pi,\\Pi'=g\\Pi\\), remove their richest ambient lines, and retain at least \\(\\eta_0\\ell\\) matches, \\(\\eta_0=\\eta_2/2\\). Thus both effective plane sizes exceed \\(\\eta_0\\ell\\). Bucket them by \\(h,h'\\). Lemma \\(19.2\\) gives \\(O(m^4/(h^2h'^2))\\) plane pairs. Proposition \\(4\\) bounds planar restrictions by \\(O((hh')^{3/2}/\\ell^2)\\), and each extends spatially in at most two ways. Summing gives\n\\[\nO(m^4/\\ell^3)\\le O(m^3/\\ell^{3/2}).\n\\]\nBoth orientations are included.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.4", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.5\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(1\\le r\\le N\\), each cloud of at most \\(N\\) points in \\(\\mathbb R^3\\) admits a partition of a containing half-open cube into at most \\(19r\\) regions, each a half-open dyadic cube or a cube minus one strict dyadic …", "statement": "<p>For \\(1\\le r\\le N\\), each cloud of at most \\(N\\) points in \\(\\mathbb R^3\\) admits a partition of a containing half-open cube into at most \\(19r\\) regions, each a half-open dyadic cube or a cube minus one strict dyadic descendant, each containing at most \\(8N/r\\) points.</p><p>For any two such partitions and any rigid motion \\(g\\), at most \\(K_{\\rm part}r\\) region pairs satisfy \\(g(R)\\cap R'\\ne\\varnothing\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\mu=N/r\\). Subdivide cubes with more than \\(\\mu\\) points. The finite tree of heavy cubes has fewer than \\(r\\) leaves and at most one fewer branching vertices. Along maximal one-heavy-child chains, group consecutive differences until their mass reaches \\(\\mu\\). Completed runs have mass in \\([\\mu,8\\mu)\\); at most one residual run per chain remains. Each run is one cube minus a descendant. Add light children of branching vertices and heavy leaves. There are at most \\(r+9(B+L)\\le19r\\) regions.</p><p>If a region has outer side \\(S\\), every \\(x\\) in it and \\(0&lt;s\\le S\\) admits a measurable subset within \\(B(x,\\sqrt3s)\\) of volume at least \\((s/4)^3\\): choose the dyadic subcube containing \\(x\\) at scale between \\(s/2\\) and \\(s\\); if it meets the hole, a child not leading to the hole supplies the set.</p><p>Charge each intersecting region pair to its smaller outer side. For a fixed charged region of side \\(s\\), each larger partner supplies such a volume subset near an intersection point. The partner sets are disjoint and all lie in a ball of radius \\(O(s)\\). Volume comparison bounds the number of partners absolutely. Sum over the \\(O(r)\\) regions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.5", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.6\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For ordered pairs of distinct source and target affine lines in \\(\\mathbb R^3\\):", "statement": "<p>For ordered pairs of distinct source and target affine lines in \\(\\mathbb R^3\\):</p><p>If the source pair is nonparallel, at most eight rigid motions give the prescribed line images.</p><p>If it is parallel, the target pair must be parallel at the same positive separation. There are at most four orthogonal parts, each with an affine one-dimensional translation family. If each specified line contains at most \\(H\\) cloud points, and \\(w_i(g)\\) is its full line-match weight, then\n\\[\n\\sum_gw_1(g)w_2(g)\\le4H^3.\n\\]\nThe positive-product support is finite.</p>", "proof": "<p>For nonparallel unit directions, \\(Sa_i=\\pm b_i\\) gives four sign choices and at most two extensions on the perpendicular line. Each translation is the intersection of two nonparallel affine lines in translation space.</p><p>For parallel pairs, let \\(d,e\\) be their ordered perpendicular separation vectors. Then \\(Sa=\\pm b\\), \\(Sd=e\\), giving at most four orthogonal parts; translations are determined modulo the target direction. For each scalar translation parameter \\(\\tau\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_\\tau w_1(\\tau)\\le H^2,\\qquad w_2(\\tau)\\le H.\n\\]\nSum over the at most four parts.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.6", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.7\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A family of at most \\(L\\) distinct spatial lines has \\(O(L^{3/2})\\) ordered parallel pairs at any prescribed positive separation.", "statement": "<p>A family of at most \\(L\\) distinct spatial lines has \\(O(L^{3/2})\\) ordered parallel pairs at any prescribed positive separation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Intersect a parallelism class of \\(q\\) lines with a perpendicular plane. Its separation graph is a planar fixed-distance graph. Two vertices have at most two common neighbors. If it has \\(e\\) edges and degrees \\(d_z\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_z\\binom{d_z}{2}\\le2\\binom q2,\\qquad\n(2e)^2\\le q\\sum_zd_z^2\\le3q^3.\n\\]\nThus there are \\(O(q^{3/2})\\) ordered pairs. Sum over classes using \\(\\sum q_j^{3/2}\\le(\\sum q_j)^{3/2}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 20", "orig": "Proposition \\(2.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Work in the sequence setting of Proposition \\(6\\).", "statement": "<p>Work in the sequence setting of Proposition \\(6\\). After passing to a subsequence, there is a symmetric selection \\(\\mathcal S\\) of at least \\(cN^2\\) unordered physical pairs such that, in every star at \\(p\\), the evaluations of homogeneous degree\n\\[\nD=\\lfloor A\\rfloor\n\\]\npolynomials at the selected displacement directions are linearly independent. Every selected star therefore has homogeneous separators of degree \\(D\\), and at most one selected partner in each projective direction.</p>", "proof": "<p>For any subset \\(T\\) of partners in one star, counting physical partners even at repeated directions, form its degree-\\(D\\) projective envelope. Assign directions to containing components and group by dimension \\(j\\), total degree \\(a_j\\). Lemma \\(20.1\\) bounds assigned partners by \\(Ca_jA^j\\), and Proposition \\(1\\) gives envelope rank at least \\(c_da_jD^j\\). There are at most \\(d\\) groups and \\(D\\asymp A\\), so\n\\[\nH_T(D)\\ge c_1|T|\n\\]\nuniformly for every subset.</p><p>Take a maximal undirected edge subset whose evaluations are independent at both endpoints. Every omitted edge is blocked at some endpoint, where its vector lies in the span of the selected vectors. Charge it there. If selected degree is \\(s_p\\), the charged vectors have rank at most \\(s_p\\), hence number at most \\(s_p/c_1\\). Summing bounds omitted edges by \\(2/c_1\\) times selected edges. Positive density follows. Surjectivity of the star evaluation map gives separators; proportional direction vectors cannot both be independent.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 20.1", "orig": "Proposition \\(2.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the same setting, a symmetric graph \\(G\\) with at least \\(cN^2\\) edges can be selected so that, for every center \\(p\\) and irreducible projective \\(Y\\subset\\mathbb P^{d-1}\\) of dimension \\(a\\) …", "statement": "<p>In the same setting, a symmetric graph \\(G\\) with at least \\(cN^2\\) edges can be selected so that, for every center \\(p\\) and irreducible projective \\(Y\\subset\\mathbb P^{d-1}\\) of dimension \\(a\\),\n\\[\n\\#\\{q:\\{p,q\\}\\in G,\\ \\pi_p(q)\\in Y\\}\n\\le C\\deg(Y)A^a.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First remove pairs on lines with more than a large fixed \\(H\\) sample points. Rich-line summation costs\n\\[\nO(N^2/H)+o(N^2).\n\\]\nAt each star greedily remove every planar-spanning irreducible curve containing more than \\(KA\\deg C\\) remaining partners. The recorded curves have total degree at most \\(N/(KA)\\). Keep a pair only if it survives both endpoints. If no fixed \\(H,K\\) left positive density on a subsequence, choose \\(H,K\\to\\infty\\) slowly; recorded curves would contradict Proposition \\(15\\). Fix a positive-density subsequence.</p><p>Induct on \\(a\\). For \\(a=0\\), use the light-line cap. Let \\(U\\) be the real span of counted displacements, of dimension \\(\\ell\\). If one radius class has \\(L\\ge2M\\) distinct projections to \\(U\\), choose representatives \\(z_i\\). Each counted \\(q\\) has at least \\(L^2/(2M)\\) ordered equal-distance pairs among them. Since they have equal radius about \\(p\\),\n\\[\n|q-z_i|=|q-z_j|\n\\implies(q-p)\\cdot(z_i-z_j)=0.\n\\]\nThis hyperplane section is proper on \\(Y\\), since its normal has nonzero projection to \\(U\\). Induction and Bézout bound each section by \\(C\\deg(Y)A^{a-1}\\). Double counting and \\(M\\le A\\) prove the bound.</p><p>Otherwise the full sample has \\(O(M^2)\\) fibers of fixed radius and projection to \\(U\\). If \\(\\ell\\ge4\\), lower-dimensional induction, the circle bound, or the two-point bound in residual dimension at most one give total \\(O(M^{d/2})\\), contradicting \\(N/M^{d/2}\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>For \\(\\ell\\le3\\) and \\(a\\ge2\\), proper-flat occupancy gives \\(O(A^2)\\); if the span is all of space, \\(d=3\\) and \\(N=A^{3/2}\\) suffices. For \\(a=1\\), either \\(Y\\subset\\mathbb P(U_{\\mathbb C})\\), when greedy curve removal applies, or the intersection is at most \\(\\deg Y\\) points, handled by the light-line cap.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 21", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In the sequence setting of Proposition \\(6\\), take \\(\\mathcal S\\) from Proposition \\(20\\), and a sufficiently generic rotated copy \\(Q=RP\\).", "statement": "<p>In the sequence setting of Proposition \\(6\\), take \\(\\mathcal S\\) from Proposition \\(20\\), and a sufficiently generic rotated copy \\(Q=RP\\). There is a nonempty retained graph \\(\\mathcal H\\) on a subset of \\(P\\times Q\\) such that\n\\[\n\\delta(\\mathcal H)\\ge cN^2/M,\\qquad\n\\delta(\\mathcal H)/A^{d-1}\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nIts vertices correspond to distinct affine pair flats\n\\[\nF_{p,q}=\\{(Z,c):Zv_0+c=y_0\\},\\qquad\nv_0=(p+q)/2,\\quad y_0=(p-q)/2,\\quad Z^t=-Z.\n\\]\nPut \\(s_0=d(d-1)/2\\). These flats satisfy the global and local concentration estimates of Lemmas \\(21.2\\) and \\(21.3\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>After the deletion in Lemma \\(21.1\\), let \\(E_0\\) be the edge count. Repeatedly delete vertices of degree below \\(E_0/(2N^2)\\). This removes fewer than \\(E_0/2\\) edges, leaving a nonempty graph with\n\\[\n\\delta(\\mathcal H)\\ge cN^2/M.\n\\]\nSince \\(N^2=A^d\\), its ratio to \\(A^{d-1}\\) is at least \\(cA/M\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>Selected stars retain their independence and separators under restriction. Pair flats have dimension \\(s_0\\). They are distinct: at \\(Z=0\\) equality recovers \\(y_0\\), and equality for all skew \\(Z\\) recovers \\(v_0\\). Subtracting neighboring pair equations gives the local equation in Lemma \\(21.3\\). Lemmas \\(21.2\\) and \\(21.3\\) establish the required concentration properties.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 21.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The graph initially joining \\((p,q)\\) to \\((p',q')\\) when \\( \\{p,p'\\}\\in\\mathcal S,\\quad \\{q,q'\\}\\in R\\mathcal S,\\quad |p-p'|=|q-q'| \\) has at least \\(cN^4/M\\) edges.", "statement": "<p>The graph initially joining \\((p,q)\\) to \\((p',q')\\) when\n\\[\n\\{p,p'\\}\\in\\mathcal S,\\quad\n\\{q,q'\\}\\in R\\mathcal S,\\quad\n|p-p'|=|q-q'|\n\\]\nhas at least \\(cN^4/M\\) edges.</p><p>In dimension three, deleting every edge whose endpoints are matches of one isometry with at least \\(C_{\\rm rich}A\\) matches costs \\(o(N^4/M)\\). Afterward no vertex has \\(C_{\\rm rich}A\\) neighbors which, together with it, are matches of one spatial isometry.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(s_\\rho\\) counts selected unordered pairs of length \\(\\rho\\), the edge count is\n\\[\n2\\sum_\\rho s_\\rho^2\\ge2|\\mathcal S|^2/M\\ge cN^4/M.\n\\]\nFor \\(d=3\\), Proposition \\(19\\), transported from \\(P\\) to \\(Q\\), bounds deletion by\n\\[\n\\sum_{k_g\\ge C_{\\rm rich}A}\\binom{k_g}{2}\n\\le K_{\\rm rich}N^4/(2A)=o(N^4/M).\n\\]\nOverlaps only lower actual cost. Any surviving prohibited neighbor collection would belong to a motion above the deleted threshold.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 21.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the retained global pair flats, every irreducible variety \\(V\\) of dimension \\(s_0+h\\), \\(0\\le h\\le d\\), contains at most \\( C\\deg(V)A^h \\) of them.", "statement": "<p>For the retained global pair flats, every irreducible variety \\(V\\) of dimension \\(s_0+h\\), \\(0\\le h\\le d\\), contains at most\n\\[\nC\\deg(V)A^h\n\\]\nof them. Here \\(h=d\\) is the ambient endpoint.</p>", "proof": "<p>Identify \\((Z,c)\\) with skew forms on \\(\\mathbb C^{d+1}\\) via\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}Z&amp;c\\\\-c^t&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe pair parameter is\n\\[\n[v:y]=[(v_0,1):(y_0,-v_0\\cdot y_0)].\n\\]\nThe evaluation equation is exactly the pair-flat equation, including the last coordinate by skew symmetry.</p><p>Products of the physical separators in \\(p,q\\), expressed in \\(v_0,y_0\\) and homogenized by the last \\(v\\)-coordinate, give homogeneous separators of degree \\(O(M)\\le O(A)\\). In a common maximal-isotropic projective plane, polarization of the real representatives gives\n\\[\n(v_0-v_0')\\cdot(y_0-y_0')=0,\n\\qquad |p-p'|^2=|q-q'|^2.\n\\]\nThus \\(p\\) is injective on those parameters, so there are at most \\(N\\le A^{d-1}\\). The total number is at most \\(N^2=A^d\\). Apply Proposition \\(18\\)(a) with \\(e=d+1,r=d\\). The same-dimensional and ambient endpoints follow directly.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 21.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.5\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On a fixed retained \\(F_{p,q}\\), a neighbor with \\( u=p'-p,\\qquad w=q'-q \\) cuts out \\( Z(u+w)=u-w. \\) Every nonzero polynomial on \\(F_{p,q}\\) of degree at most \\(A\\) contains at most \\(CA^{d-1}\\) such neighbor flats in …", "statement": "<p>On a fixed retained \\(F_{p,q}\\), a neighbor with\n\\[\nu=p'-p,\\qquad w=q'-q\n\\]\ncuts out\n\\[\nZ(u+w)=u-w.\n\\]\nEvery nonzero polynomial on \\(F_{p,q}\\) of degree at most \\(A\\) contains at most \\(CA^{d-1}\\) such neighbor flats in its zero set.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(R\\) generically so all relevant \\(u+w\\ne0\\). The local skew model has \\(e=d,r=d-1\\), parameter\n\\[\n[v:y]=[u+w:u-w].\n\\]\nThese parameters are distinct: proportionality gives \\((u',w')=\\lambda(u,w)\\), with real \\(\\lambda\\); uniqueness in the selected physical direction forces \\(u'=u\\), hence \\(\\lambda=1,w'=w\\).</p><p>Multiply the degree-\\(D\\) star separators for \\(u,w\\), and substitute \\(u=(v+y)/2,w=(v-y)/2\\), giving homogeneous parameter separators of degree \\(O(A)\\).</p><p>For parameters in one maximal-isotropic plane, polarization gives\n\\[\nu_i\\cdot u_j=w_i\\cdot w_j.\n\\]\nPositive definiteness makes \\(u_i\\mapsto w_i\\) a well-defined real span isometry, extending to \\(S\\in O(d)\\). These neighbors and the center are matches of\n\\[\nx\\mapsto q+S(x-p).\n\\]\nFor \\(d\\ge4\\), their number is at most \\(N=A^{d/2}\\le A^{d-2}\\); for \\(d=3\\), Lemma \\(21.1\\) supplies the \\(O(A)\\) survivor cap. This is exactly the \\(O(A^{r-1})\\) isotropic hypothesis, including both orientations.</p><p>Factor the polynomial into irreducible hypersurfaces. Each has excess dimension \\(d-2=r-1\\) above a neighbor flat and lies in a degree-at-most-\\(A\\) zero set. Proposition \\(18\\)(b), summed over components of total degree at most \\(A\\), gives \\(CA^{d-1}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 22", "orig": "Proposition \\(2.6\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The retained graph and pair-flat family of Proposition \\(21\\) cannot simultaneously satisfy its minimum-degree condition and the global and local concentration bounds.", "statement": "<p>The retained graph and pair-flat family of Proposition \\(21\\) cannot simultaneously satisfy its minimum-degree condition and the global and local concentration bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal F\\) be its \\(m\\) retained pair flats, and \\(D=\\lfloor A\\rfloor\\). Form the degree-\\(D\\) envelope of their union. Assign each flat to a containing irreducible component and group components by dimensions \\(s_0+h\\), \\(0\\le h\\le d\\), with degrees \\(a_h\\). Global concentration bounds assigned flats by \\(Ca_hA^h\\). Proposition \\(1\\), applied at the envelope’s defining degree, gives\n\\[\nH_{\\bigcup\\mathcal F}(D)\\ge c\\,a_hD^{s_0+h}.\n\\]\nThere are only \\(d+1\\) groups, so\n\\[\nH_{\\bigcup\\mathcal F}(D)\\ge c_4mA^{s_0}.\n\\]</p><p>Sample each flat independently with a small fixed probability \\(\\theta&gt;0\\). With probability at least \\(1/2\\), at most \\(2\\theta m\\) are selected. Their restriction rank is at most\n\\[\n2\\theta m\\binom{D+s_0}{s_0}\n\\le C_4\\theta mA^{s_0}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\theta\\) so this is strictly below the preceding lower bound.</p><p>At every vertex, sampled neighbors are binomial of mean at least \\(\\theta\\delta(\\mathcal H)\\). Chernoff bounds the probability of fewer than half this mean by\n\\[\n\\exp(-c\\theta\\delta(\\mathcal H)).\n\\]\nA union bound over at most \\(N^2\\) vertices tends to zero, since \\(\\delta(\\mathcal H)\\gg A^{d-1}\\), a positive power of \\(N\\). Hence one sample has both small rank and more than \\(CA^{d-1}\\) sampled neighbors at every vertex.</p><p>Strict rank inequality gives a polynomial \\(f\\) of degree at most \\(D\\), vanishing on every sampled flat but nonzero on some retained flat \\(F\\). Its nonzero restriction to \\(F\\) vanishes on all distinct intersections with sampled neighbor flats. There are more than \\(CA^{d-1}\\), contradicting Lemma \\(21.3\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[2, 1, "part"], [3, 1, "part"], [4, 1, "part"], [5, 1, "part"], [6, 1, "part"], [8, 7, "part"], [9, 7, "part"], [11, 10, "part"], [12, 10, "part"], [13, 10, "part"], [14, 10, "part"], [15, 10, "part"], [16, 10, "part"], [18, 17, "part"], [19, 17, "part"], [20, 17, "part"], [21, 17, "part"], [23, 22, "part"], [24, 22, "part"], [25, 22, "part"], [26, 22, "part"], [27, 22, "part"], [30, 29, "part"], [32, 31, "part"], [33, 31, "part"], [34, 31, "part"], [36, 35, "part"], [37, 35, "part"], [38, 35, "part"], [41, 40, "part"], [43, 42, "part"], [45, 44, "part"], [46, 44, "part"], [47, 44, "part"], [49, 48, "part"], [52, 51, "part"], [54, 53, "part"], [55, 53, "part"], [57, 56, "part"], [59, 58, "part"], [60, 58, "part"], [61, 58, "part"], [62, 58, "part"], [63, 58, "part"], [64, 58, "part"], [66, 65, "part"], [68, 67, "part"], [69, 67, "part"], [70, 67, "part"]]});
